window.CHAPTERS = [{"id":"ch1","order":1,"book":"I","bookTitle":"Book I — The Marchlands Commission","num":"I","label":"Chapter I","isEpilogue":false,"date":"September 19, 2026","inFrom":330,"inTo":331,"inApprox":false,"title":"The Quiet Knives","subtitle":"September 19, 2026 session. A wax locust, a market full of centipedes, and the first job the company was ever offered.","md":"## **The Quiet Knives**\n\n*September 19, 2026 session. A wax locust, a market full of centipedes, and the first job the company was ever offered.*\n\n\n\n### **The Boy on the Crate**\n\n**Lupenor's Market** on the 26th of Neth was doing what a market does in a city ten weeks out of\ndemon hands, which is shouting. Traders had claimed whatever ground they could hold: a cart here,\na lean-to propped against a wall there, a carpet spread flat on cobbles that were old when the\ncitadel fell. Seven colours of silk. Pepper, rosemary, salt. Chickens underfoot. A board by the\nwater pump carried the week's fixed prices in chalk, and grain had been rubbed out and written in\nagain three times that month. The merchants had arrived well ahead of the residents.\n\nFour people were standing in that street who did not know one another.\n\n**Riven Maelus** was negotiating over salvage with **Wat Crake**, a broad, grey-bearded man of\nabout fifty-five with a leather patch over one eye, who dug under Drezen's walls in the taking and\nstayed on afterwards to dig the ruins out instead. Everything on Wat's rug used to be broken:\nhinges, a kettle, a lock with a new key tied to it, a mended lantern, a row of old tools gone\nbrown and oiled back into use. Riven had pulled a tea kettle and a few spoons out of an abandoned\nhouse on the South Bank, mended them, polished them, and was being offered ten copper for the lot.\nWat watches the alleys the way men do who have spent a long time underground, and he does not\nthink much of people who ask where his stock came from.\n\n**Kora Sjon** had her back to the water pump, buying pins and bone buttons from **Marit Olsk**, a\nsmall tired woman in a good grey coat gone threadbare at the cuffs, calling needles and thread off\nan upturned crate that was not hers.\n\n**Dorogh Kell** had come in from the west end to buy a birthday present. He had not settled on\nwhose. He found a spot out of the flow of traffic, because he is the tallest thing in most streets\nand people mention it.\n\n**Diddle Scribes** was losing a haggle. His master had handed him a shopping list and a promise to\nreimburse whatever he judged the goods were worth, which is one way to teach a lesson about\npeople. **Ketta Holm**, a wiry herbalist of about thirty-five who came up from Nerosyan as a camp\napothecary and stayed in Drezen because the army pays for bandages on time, had raised her price\non him because he smelled funny. He offered three copper anyway. She was surprised enough to come\ndown to four, from seven, and he walked away with his cat's whisker and a quiet private sense of\nhaving won something.\n\nThen a boy climbed onto a crate beside the water pump.\n\nHe was sixteen at most, in a tradesman's apron with something yellow and sticky down the front of\nit, and he held up a lump of wax shaped like a locust by somebody who had never looked closely at\none. Marit began shouting at him to get down before he made a fool of himself. **Jory Tallow**,\nshe called him, apprenticed to the candle-maker up the road. He had come up out of **Kenabres**\nwith her. She had bought her candles from his master's shop for years and watched him grow up\nbehind the counter, and when the city was opened in Arodus she had assumed he was dead. He was a\ngood kid, she told Kora, a little rattled in the head, and who wasn't.\n\nJory was not listening. He was addressing the market.\n\n\"Remember Kenabres. Remember how hopeless it was. Remember who won. Don't you want to be on the\nwinning side?\"\n\nDorogh, who could not live where he lives without knowing the name **Deskari**, shrugged and went\nback to his shopping with half an eye on the crate. Riven walked over for a closer look, counting\nchildren on the way, on the theory that a crowd this loud usually has somebody small working it.\nThere were three, and none of them were thieves: a gangly boy called **Aldo Fenn** in a\nPreserver's white tabard a size too large, holding a new whetstone and carrying no weapon at all;\na laundress called **Liesl Ambry** with her two-year-old son **Benno** on her hip, gnawing a\ncrust; and a seven-year-old at the baker's handcart named **Pip Venn**.\n\nJory reached his crescendo. Deskari would preserve him, and if they doubted it they could watch\nwhat Deskari would do. Then he kicked out the side of the crate he was standing on.\n\n![It was hard to take Jory seriously.](characters/jory-tallow.webp)\n\n### **Four Strangers and Four Centipedes**\n\nWhat spilled out was one rolling knot of legs that took a moment to sort itself into four giant\ncentipedes, each about as long as a man is tall and a foot and a half wide counting the parts that\nmoved. They scattered into the market and went hunting for whatever looked easiest.\n\nThe street answered the way a street does.\n\nOld **Hrenna** stayed on her upturned bucket at the brazier and held her hands out to the coals.\nShe is seventy, Sarkorian, and was carried off from a steading as a girl and kept in this city by\nthe demons who held it, and she has been free for a few weeks. There is not very much left of her.\nShe watched the whole thing with enormous patient eyes, and she did not move once, and if one of\nthem had come at her it would have had her, and she knew it.\n\n**Wat Crake** selected a length of iron from among his wares and stood slapping it into his palm,\nwaiting for anything at all to come onto his carpet. He was not frightened. He was annoyed.\n\n**Piet Harl**, a clerk in a black coat gone shiny at the elbows who kept books for a counting-house\nin Kenabres until Kenabres was opened, discovered that a clerk's velvet slippers are not running\nshoes and went down the street screaming, trailing paper out of his satchel. **Dun Ferrow**, a big\nslow porter, put down a sack of meal and spent a good part of the crisis working out whether he\nstill got paid if he ran. **Liesl Ambry**, whose husband did not come back from the taking, got\nher body between Benno and the street and did not move again.\n\nThe four strangers did something else.\n\nRiven stepped back and put a fist of blue-white light into the nearest centipede, a working that\nslid off his hand and kept the shape of it as it flew. The thing rolled up onto its front legs and\ncame down again with a noise like heavy rain on a roof, and did not change its mind about where it\nwas going. He shouted at Aldo Fenn to run, and Aldo ran, instantly and without arguing. Aldo is\nseventeen. He joined **Iomedae's Preservers** the week Drezen fell, because the Preservers are\nHarlock Greyforge's and Harlock Greyforge is the greatest man he has ever seen from a distance,\nand he has been drilled with a spear ever since by other recruits who do not know how either.\nNobody had ever given him an order in a real fight before. He has thought about it a good deal\nsince.\n\n![The first magic in this chronicle, and it did not change the centipede's mind.](images/ch1-riven-telekinetic-fist.webp)\n\nPip went under her father's handcart. The centipede went under after her.\n\nDorogh got down on his belly in the filth of the street and worked a long spear into the gap,\ntrying to reach the thing that was eating a child without putting the point through the child. It\nwas not a clean angle and it did not give him a clean result. Kora put a crossbow bolt into a\nsecond centipede from across the way. Diddle, who had come to the market to buy herbs, decided\nthat children being eaten was worth the risk, moved up along the stalls, and lobbed a vial that he\nthumbed open in the air. It burst and reduced a centipede to legs and flame. It also set fire to a\nfruit stall, which he went and stamped out afterwards.\n\nThe centipede under the cart bit Pip through the middle and she stopped moving.\n\nKora crossed twenty feet of contested street to get within reach of her, crouched to see under the\naxle, and made a sound like a chirp. There was a light so faint you had to already be looking for\nit to see it at midday. The girl's bleeding stopped. Her father, **Oskar Venn**, saw the wounds go\nstill and took it for the worst news of his life.\n\n![A light so faint you had to already be looking for it.](images/ch1-first-action.webp)\n\nDorogh killed the thing under the cart from his belly and got up. Riven killed the one Kora had\nwounded. **Hobb Garrow**, who soldiered in the Fourth Crusade and has cooked for the Preservers'\nbarracks ever since, was in the street arguing over a string of onions. He came in with his\ncleaver, took a bite and a dose of paralytic for his trouble, and stayed upright out of sheer\nindignation. The last centipede bit Jory Tallow as he tried to run from the crate he had opened,\nwhich he would later find theologically confusing.\n\nDorogh put the spear through it, and the fight was over in less time than it takes to describe.\n\nHobb then tackled Jory into the cobbles, sat on him, and shouted for the watch.\n\nRiven walked over and knocked the boy unconscious with one punch. It was a decisive blow, and\neveryone present agreed that it had looked easy.\n\n![Diddle Scribes putting out the part of it that was his own fault.](images/ch1-diddle-stamping.webp)\n\n### **A Loaf of Bread a Day**\n\nDiddle finished stamping out his own fire, crossed the street, tugged on Kora's cloak, and held a\ndull red vial up over his head.\n\nShe had bandaged what could be bandaged and had reached the end of what bandages do. She looked at\nthe goblin, and then at the scorch marks he had made and then put out, and decided that was as\ngood a character reference as anyone gets. She poured the potion into the wound. It came up in a\nred foam and the skin closed smooth under it, and Pip pushed herself onto her elbows, reached up,\nand touched Kora's beak, and did not say anything at all.\n\nOskar wanted their names. Diddle. Kora. Doro, said Dorogh, happy to help.\n\n\"I'm never going to forget you,\" Oskar told them. \"Anytime you want bread, it's on the house. A\nloaf a day for the rest of your lives.\"\n\nIt is worth knowing what he was offering. Oskar Venn came up from Nerosyan behind the supply\nwagons a fortnight after the city fell, a widower with a child and a trade an army needs. He rents\na cold oven in an empty house two streets over. He is a narrow, stooped man who apologises to\nlarge people before they have said anything, and the one thing in the world he is proud of is that\nhis is the first bread baked in Drezen in seventy years.\n\nRiven, who had spent the fight in the open and the aftermath under a hood, went to help put out\nthe last of the flames and gave his name to nobody in a uniform.\n\nThe watch arrived from both ends of the street and converged on the one person in the market who\nhad been paying complete attention. **Bruna Aske** sells pots and pans and ladles off a rug, and\nkeeps the only honest scales in Lupenor's Market, which is a thing she has had a row with every\ntrader on the street to establish. She had spent the entire incident standing on that rug holding\nher brass scales to her chest with both hands, declining to move off it for anybody, watching.\n\nHer account was accurate down to the antenna that had landed in her good silverware. She gave the\nwatch the goblin's bomb and the goblin putting the fire out, both. She reported that the bird\nwoman had cast something she could not name, which seemed to have worked, because the child was up\nand running. She is not on anybody's side. She is on the side of the scales.\n\nThe watch asked where the cultist had gone. The garrison cook, Bruna said, meaning Hobb, had\ncarried him off over his shoulder to **Mira's**. The watchmen nodded as though that settled the\nmatter and went that way.\n\nA few minutes later somebody started calling silk again.\n\nRiven picked up the wax locust, which nobody had thought to collect. It was beeswax, scented for\nlavender, with thumbprints still in it where the boy had pressed and pulled and twisted it into\nshape. It was an ordinary lump of wax made by hand by somebody who was trying.\n\nThey followed Marit to the candle shop, where a short round man with a walrus moustache heard what\nhis apprentice had done, said the boy had not been right since Kenabres, and shut up shop to go\nand speak for him. He had noticed Jory slipping off somewhere the last few days. He did not pay\nhim enough to go drinking, and the boy had not come back drunk, so he had left it alone.\n\nNobody bought a candle.\n\n### **Two Pints and a Song**\n\nThey went for a drink instead, at a middling tavern called the **Two Pints and a Song**, and\nintroduced themselves properly for the first time: a half-orc in padded armour and plain wire\nspectacles, apologising for his size before anybody complained of it; a goblin who took his hood\noff once he was warm; a tengu with a raven's head and talons under a light grey robe, who watches\nmore than she speaks; and a tiefling with ram's horns and a staff he never quite set down.\nSomebody observed that they were a diverse bunch. Somebody else had fetched a bar stool over from\nthe counter so that Diddle could sit at the table rather than under it. Nobody made anything of\nit.\n\nDiddle lives here. He came up with his master and does not regard Drezen as his natural\nenvironment, which explains a good deal about him. Kora is from the other side of the world and\nkeeps a small fortune-telling shop on the edge of the city, where most of the work is legitimate.\nRiven travels from place to place looking for libraries.\n\nKora's drink tipped over into her lap. Her hands were nowhere near it. She did not look surprised.\nShe started cleaning it up. Riven, who had spent the morning throwing invisible fists at\ncentipedes, filed the moment away and said nothing. Dorogh offered to buy her another.\n\n![A half-orc, a goblin, a tengu, and a tiefling walk into a bar...](images/ch1-two-pints-and-a-song.webp)\n\nThere is no public library in Drezen. There are said to be whole rooms of books inside the\ncitadel, available to privileged individuals.\n\nThen a boy of about fourteen came in at the door, looked the room over, decided, and walked to\ntheir table.\n\n\"The nightingale enters through the side door,\" he said, and looked from face to face, hoping\nsomebody knew what that meant.\n\n\"The blade is locked,\" said Riven.\n\n\"Right this way.\"\n\n### **The Wall of Red String**\n\nThe side door was in an alley off the market, and the peephole was at eye level, and the woman\nbehind it wanted a password.\n\n\"Password,\" said Riven.\n\n\"That's a horrible password,\" she said, and let him in.\n\nThe room behind the door had a wall of paper in it. Floor plans. Scraps of parchment and cloth.\nCramped writing that ran margin to margin as though it were the last paper in the world. Long\nlooping hands. Some of it in code. All of it strung together with red wool running in every\ndirection. The notes had not come out of one person's desk. One page among them was a diary entry\nwritten by a little girl. It was not signed, and it was not in Mira's hand.\n\nJory Tallow was tied to a chair in the middle of it with one black eye and a bandaged bite.\n\n**Mira Thistledance** keeps **Lupenor Celest's** market while Lupenor rides west. She is a half-elf\nwith green eyes and brown hair threaded with fine silver chains, a lute swinging behind her on its\nstrap and a wax tablet in one hand covered edge to edge in cramped notes and arrows, and she has\ndecided, with total delight, that her real position is spymaster of Drezen. She had watched the\nwhole thing in the market from a second-floor window. She had already interrogated the boy. What\nshe wanted now was to watch the four of them work.\n\nSo Riven pulled his hood back, put his horns in the boy's entire field of vision, and observed\nthat the only reliable way to purify a thing touched by demons is to set it on fire. The colour\nwent out of Jory's face. Kora put a hand on his shoulder and told him to be calm and say what\nhappened. Dorogh folded his arms and cracked his knuckles and did his best to look like hired\nmuscle.\n\nIt worked, in the sense that the boy said everything he knew in one breath.\n\nHe had wanted to be on the winning side. He had been in Kenabres in Arodus when the city was\nopened, and had seen the thing in the sky above it, and had watched the crusade fail to stop it.\nHe was certain Deskari would win and would crush anyone who was not his, so he had gone looking\nfor people who worshipped Deskari, following rumours down to a scrapyard on the South Bank where\ncultists were said to gather. They were cultists. They served some other demon lord entirely. They\nlaughed at him and sent him home.\n\nSo he had gone down into the cellars of the abandoned houses on the South Bank alone, and caught\ngiant centipedes, and kept them in a crate, and fed them rats until he had enough of them to stage\nan exhibition in the marketplace and get his god's attention.\n\nRiven remarked that being pious had never saved anybody's parents that he knew of.\n\n\"At least you had parents,\" Jory said. \"My father's face was dissolved by a giant fly. I watched\nit happen.\"\n\nSomebody pointed out that he had very nearly killed a seven-year-old girl.\n\n\"Deskari spared her, obviously,\" said Jory. \"I don't know why the centipedes attacked me, though.\"\n\nMira did not want to hand him to **Iomedae's Preservers**, on the grounds that they would hang\nhim, and she did not care for packing him off to be some other town's problem either. The\nsuggestion that came out of the room was **Sarenrae**, who is a goddess of redemption, who keeps a\nchurch in the city, and who might conceivably take an apprentice. Mira shrugged and said she would\nask, and that she hoped they would take him, because she hated to have to put him down. Then she\nrang a bell, and a bald man of about fifty who had spent most of those summers carrying heavy\nthings came in, picked up the chair with the boy still tied to it, and carried both out the way he\nhad come.\n\n\"I have plans for that boy,\" Mira said. \"Maybe we can use him as a double agent.\" Then she pulled\na wax tablet off her desk. \"But first. Code names.\"\n\nOperatives in the ballads are birds, so Diddle was to be Wren, on the grounds that wrens are very\nloud and very small. Somebody observed that bird names might land badly with a tengu in the room.\nMira conceded the point instantly, flipped the tablet over, and read off the alternates from the\nback.\n\nDiddle was Tomato, because he throws things. Dorogh was Yam. Kora was Carrot. Riven was Cabbage.\n\nDiddle asked whether he could be Pickle instead. He was not Pickle.\n\n![Operative Cabbage, Operative Carrot, Operative Tomato and Operative Yam.](images/ch1-operative-cabbage.webp)\n\nThe pitch followed. She is the spymaster of Drezen, she said, dropping her voice to a volume that\ncarried, and she has been looking a long time for an elite strike team of master infiltrators, and\nthis morning in the marketplace she found one. She had done a little checking. They were\nunaffiliated. They had no connection to her or to the city. They were completely deniable.\n\n\"You mean expendable.\"\n\n\"I hadn't thought of it that way.\"\n\nWhat was in it for them, Diddle asked.\n\n\"Glory. And a steady paycheck.\"\n\n\"How is it glory if it's undercover?\"\n\n\"It's very quiet glory. Few people will know. Very well-connected people.\" She brightened. \"And\neventually you'll all be dead, and they'll sing songs about you.\"\n\nThere is a cult embedded in the South Bank, at a scrapyard on **Cinder Row**, where people go in\nand do not always come out, and where sometimes more come out than went in. Mira wanted a head\ncount, the name of the demon lord they serve, and their plans, and she would pay a hundred and\nfifty gold for it, more for better intelligence. Her operational proposal was that the company\ninfiltrate the place disguised as a troupe of acrobats. When that landed poorly she offered monks.\n\nShe was also still hunting for the right name for the strike force. She tried **the Quiet Knives**\non them, and made the whole room say it with her, and did not seem to love it either. She offered\nthe Market Shadows. She offered Operation Nightingale, and then took it straight back: that was a\nmission name, she said, and she was saving it for a mission worth the having. Nobody told her.\nSomebody countered with the Marketplace Irregulars. It was agreed that the question could wait.\n\nThey signed on for the one job, as a trial. Riven asked for library access as part of the terms,\nand Mira thought research was a wonderful idea and tore a sheet of parchment in half and wrote him\na note on it. Then they worked out a signal system, and she took it down on the wax tablet as they\nwent. A red flag tied to the water spout in the market meant she needed to talk. A blue flag meant\nthey did. A purple flag meant she needed to talk, purple being nearly red. Any other flag on the\nwater spout meant that somebody had tied a flag to the water spout.\n\nSomebody asked what had become of her last team.\n\n\"There hasn't been a last team. This is my first strike team.\" She beamed at them. \"You're doing\ngreat, by the way.\"\n\n### **The Bearer Is Singular**\n\nThe note worked, in the narrowest possible sense.\n\nThe citadel gates were shut for the night and six guards were on them, and they watched a hooded\ntiefling with a stick come up the causeway after dark, followed by an upright black bird, a\ngoblin, and a half-orc. The guard who stepped forward to take the note was either the most senior\nor the least fortunate. He flipped it open, looked at it, and turned to the others.\n\n\"Anybody here read?\"\n\nOne of them could. The note entitled the bearer to full access to the library at the citadel and\nwas signed Mira Thistledance, and the guards took the view that this meant precisely one person.\n\nAn argument followed. The company put it to the gate that they were one unit, and that a unit\nholds a note collectively. The literate guard observed that bearer is singular and not plural. The\nothers noted that the four of them were not of a height, were not of a race, and did not have the\nsame number of fingers. A man was sent in to ask a sergeant. The sergeant said no. Kora threw a\nvoice behind them suggesting that somebody in authority had cleared it, and the guards agreed that\na voice had spoken and did not believe a word of it.\n\nDiddle went home. His master was still waiting on the morning's ingredients, and Diddle has\noutlived the average goblin by a comfortable margin and intends to go on doing it.\n\nSo Riven went in alone, and Kora walked back across the city in the dark and knocked on Mira's\nside door, and Mira opened the peephole and said, \"Agent Carrot. What are you doing here?\" and\nwrote out two more notes without asking a single question, because research is wonderful.\n\nThe library held four or five hundred books, none of them light and none of them recent. They were\nthe sort of thing a long-dead wizard writes after visiting somewhere he should not have gone, and\nthey were no use whatsoever for finding out who is in your city this week. For a demon lord they\nwere excellent.\n\nDeskari is the Lord of the Locust Host. The Worldwound is not simply a hole in the world. The far\nside of it opens into his realm, so that a man may walk out of Golarion into Deskari's country\nwithout troubling the Abyss in between, and much of what lives in the Worldwound belongs to him.\nMadness is among his domains, and very few humans worship him on purpose. Those who do are\ngenerally a little crazy, unless they are a lot crazy.\n\n\"So we're dealing with crazy people,\" said somebody.\n\n\"We already were,\" said somebody else.\n\nAfter Deskari, the second most written-about demon lord on those shelves was **Baphomet**, which\nsaid something about whoever had assembled the collection. Dorogh spent an hour on the question of\nwhether the two were rivals, which would have been useful, and established instead that they are\nallies, which was not. He came away with Baphomet's holy symbols: a pentacle with a goat's head at\nthe centre, two backward curves meeting like a letter, and a labyrinth.\n\nAn older woman at the next table told them to keep it down. She was studying. What she was\nstudying was none of their concern.\n\nThey went home to sleep on it, and agreed to meet at the alchemist's shop after breakfast.\n\n### **A Copper a Pound**\n\nThey met at the alchemist's shop after breakfast, where Diddle's master opened up to find three\nstrangers on his step asking for his goblin, and elected not to ask about it. Dorogh greeted\nDiddle as Pickle. Diddle corrected him: Tomato. Operative Tomato. Dorogh observed that Pickle was\nwhat he had asked for. It was settled that he could have both, on the grounds that Mira was not\ngoing to check.\n\nThe South Bank is the worst part of Drezen and nobody in Drezen argues the point. It is where the\nslaves were housed during the occupation. It has no reliable water, no arable ground, no decent\nroads, no street lamps, no lamp oil, no heating oil, and very little watch. Nobody sleeps rough\ndown there, because there are more abandoned houses than there are people to fill them and some of\nthe roofs are still mostly on. Rubbish goes at the side of the road. The Ahari has been dry for\nmonths, so you cross it on a bridge over nothing.\n\nThe 27th of Neth was cold enough to see your breath.\n\nThe scrapyard on Cinder Row was a fair-sized house with a yard behind it, walled in an eight-foot\nfence of uneven planks. There was a red arrow slapped onto the boards pointing round into the\nalley, a sandwich board that said SCRAP, and a sign on the gate that said BEWARE OF DOGS.\n\nThere were two, and they were the one thing in that yard that could have been settled kindly.\nRiven found the first of them by its nose in a knothole and, seeing no better idea on offer,\nreached through the fence with a spectral hand and rubbed its belly. The dog lost its mind. The\nsecond dog joined in. Between the yelping and the chain-rattling the company established that the\nyard held two large animals of the same line, one younger and the other missing half an ear and an\nentire leg, and that they were not so much fighting as disagreeing. Through the gaps in the planks\nthey also established a pile of salvaged lumber, a pile of masonry sorted by colour, and a heap of\nrust.\n\nDorogh knocked at the office door as an honest man looking for work. The man who opened it had a\nscrap hook in one hand and a hangover behind his eyes, and quoted a copper a pound for good steel,\nwith everything under that open to negotiation. They took lumber, stone, wagon parts, machine\nparts. Tinkerers came by looking to repair things. Pound on the back gate when you come, he said,\nand he would chain the dogs and weigh it out.\n\nIt was a scrapyard. It was an entirely functional, unglamorous scrapyard.\n\nInked on the inside of the man's wrist was a labyrinth. Kora saw it, and said nothing at all until\nthey were well down the alley.\n\nThey talked it over somewhere private. Two men at least, sleeping where they worked, which made a\nnight approach worse rather than better. Two dogs. A tattoo. The word of a half-mad boy who had\nbeen turned away at that gate. Dorogh, who knows the difference between a load-bearing wall and\nsalvageable lumber, spent an hour building a sled out of a door and two lengths of mismatched\nrope, while the others chipped sixty pounds of ceramic tile off the wall of what had been a\nbathhouse and gathered a hundred pounds of short planks. It looked like an honest haul because it\nwas one.\n\nThe plan they agreed on was to go in as customers and take the yard without killing anybody, on\nthe grounds that the city watch takes a keen interest in murder and none whatsoever in a\nscrapyard.\n\nThe dogs heard the sled coming from thirty feet out. The company heard somebody inside kick one of\nthem quiet.\n\nThe gate opened on a worker complaining that it had only been an hour and he had barely slept, and\nthen giving up and waving them in. He set a scale up by the wall and began turning over the tile\nwith his hook. The second man went to sit with the dogs. Both of them had the same labyrinth on\nthem, one on the upper arm and one on the back of the wrist. Diddle admired the tattoo out loud.\nThe man squinted at him, said \"What of it,\" and went back to testing the chains. Diddle then asked\nwhen the scale had last been calibrated, and the man offered to wait while he went home for his\nown weights, and spat on the ground.\n\nKora drifted along the piles, browsing, until she could see through the door into the house: a\ntable, a hearth, a pair of chairs, and two more men eating breakfast. Riven found a heap big\nenough to crouch behind and put an armour of force around himself where nobody could see him do\nit.\n\nThen Dorogh, with two men in front of him and no way to ask the others whether the moment had\ncome, decided that it had, and bolted through the door.\n\nHe came through it at a dead run behind the shield, roaring, and swung it at the head of a man who\nhad his back turned and was expecting nothing at all, and missed, and spilled his coffee.\n\n![Operative Yam springs the ambush.](images/ch1-dorogh-shield-bash.webp)\n\nAfter that it went the way these things go.\n\nDiddle stepped back, judged the distance, and put a bomb into the worker by the kennel, which\ndropped him at once, set fire to the lumber pile he had been standing on, and singed both dogs,\nwho began to scream in their chains. The man at the scale finally looked up from his weights to\nfind an explosion behind him, a woman shrieking in the house, and his customers gone. He came at\nDorogh with the hook and missed.\n\nA woman came out of a side room, heavily pregnant, asking what all this was. Riven laid a working\nof exhaustion on her and she sagged back into the doorway and held it.\n\nHer husband is **Hesk Dolvan**, a scrap dealer past fifty in a good coat gone shiny at the elbows,\nwith a scrap merchant's hands and a scrap merchant's habit of pricing whatever is in front of him\nbefore he decides about it. He stood up from the breakfast table, took a hatchet off his belt that\nwas more tool than weapon, said \"Stay away from my wife,\" and put it into Riven, who went down in\nthe doorway and stayed down.\n\nSomewhere close by, a baby started screaming. Something thumped against the door **Alia Dolvan**\nwas holding shut, and the handle rattled, and she did not let it open.\n\nKora put a crossbow bolt into the man with the black chin whiskers. Diddle went past everybody's\nlegs at a dead run, announcing a medic coming through, knelt in the doorway, and poured his last\npotion into Riven, who came back to himself face down on the floor of a stranger's house. It was\nthe second he had spent in two days and the last one he had. Kora got in beside Dorogh and closed\nhis wounds while a hatchet was being swung at the pair of them.\n\nDorogh killed the man he had failed to knock out.\n\nThe worker with the bolts in his back gave up on the yard, ran inside past the company, and began\nfumbling at a trapdoor set into the middle of the floor. It was chained shut. He did not have the\nkey. He went out the far door instead and kept going.\n\nAlia got past Riven while he was too hurt to stop her, went into her own kitchen, found a knife\nwhere she knew it would be, and began working up the courage to use it on him.\n\nA door opened. **Sera Dolvan**, who is twelve, looked out at the burning yard, at the man lying\nface down across the table, at the half-orc with the morningstar and the goblin at his feet, and\nthen closed the door again. Behind it were her brother **Corin**, who is nine and was shouting for\nhis mother, and the baby in its crib. The dogs were still in their chains and the lumber was still\nburning, and nobody had yet said the word Baphomet out loud inside that house.\n\nThe company had come to count cultists. What they had found so far was a family, a trapdoor that\nnobody was allowed to open, and a fight they had started themselves.\n\n*Session of September 19, 2026.*","text":"The Quiet Knives September 19, 2026 session. A wax locust, a market full of centipedes, and the first job the company was ever offered. The Boy on the Crate Lupenor's Market on the 26th of Neth was doing what a market does in a city ten weeks out of demon hands, which is shouting. Traders had claimed whatever ground they could hold: a cart here, a lean-to propped against a wall there, a carpet spread flat on cobbles that were old when the citadel fell. Seven colours of silk. Pepper, rosemary, salt. Chickens underfoot. A board by the water pump carried the week's fixed prices in chalk, and grain had been rubbed out and written in again three times that month. The merchants had arrived well ahead of the residents. Four people were standing in that street who did not know one another. Riven Maelus was negotiating over salvage with Wat Crake , a broad, grey-bearded man of about fifty-five with a leather patch over one eye, who dug under Drezen's walls in the taking and stayed on afterwards to dig the ruins out instead. Everything on Wat's rug used to be broken: hinges, a kettle, a lock with a new key tied to it, a mended lantern, a row of old tools gone brown and oiled back into use. Riven had pulled a tea kettle and a few spoons out of an abandoned house on the South Bank, mended them, polished them, and was being offered ten copper for the lot. Wat watches the alleys the way men do who have spent a long time underground, and he does not think much of people who ask where his stock came from. Kora Sjon had her back to the water pump, buying pins and bone buttons from Marit Olsk , a small tired woman in a good grey coat gone threadbare at the cuffs, calling needles and thread off an upturned crate that was not hers. Dorogh Kell had come in from the west end to buy a birthday present. He had not settled on whose. He found a spot out of the flow of traffic, because he is the tallest thing in most streets and people mention it. Diddle Scribes was losing a haggle. His master had handed him a shopping list and a promise to reimburse whatever he judged the goods were worth, which is one way to teach a lesson about people. Ketta Holm , a wiry herbalist of about thirty-five who came up from Nerosyan as a camp apothecary and stayed in Drezen because the army pays for bandages on time, had raised her price on him because he smelled funny. He offered three copper anyway. She was surprised enough to come down to four, from seven, and he walked away with his cat's whisker and a quiet private sense of having won something. Then a boy climbed onto a crate beside the water pump. He was sixteen at most, in a tradesman's apron with something yellow and sticky down the front of it, and he held up a lump of wax shaped like a locust by somebody who had never looked closely at one. Marit began shouting at him to get down before he made a fool of himself. Jory Tallow , she called him, apprenticed to the candle-maker up the road. He had come up out of Kenabres with her. She had bought her candles from his master's shop for years and watched him grow up behind the counter, and when the city was opened in Arodus she had assumed he was dead. He was a good kid, she told Kora, a little rattled in the head, and who wasn't. Jory was not listening. He was addressing the market. \"Remember Kenabres. Remember how hopeless it was. Remember who won. Don't you want to be on the winning side?\" Dorogh, who could not live where he lives without knowing the name Deskari , shrugged and went back to his shopping with half an eye on the crate. Riven walked over for a closer look, counting children on the way, on the theory that a crowd this loud usually has somebody small working it. There were three, and none of them were thieves: a gangly boy called Aldo Fenn in a Preserver's white tabard a size too large, holding a new whetstone and carrying no weapon at all; a laundress called Liesl Ambry with her two-year-old son Benno on her hip, gnawing a crust; and a seven-year-old at the baker's handcart named Pip Venn . Jory reached his crescendo. Deskari would preserve him, and if they doubted it they could watch what Deskari would do. Then he kicked out the side of the crate he was standing on. It was hard to take Jory seriously. Four Strangers and Four Centipedes What spilled out was one rolling knot of legs that took a moment to sort itself into four giant centipedes, each about as long as a man is tall and a foot and a half wide counting the parts that moved. They scattered into the market and went hunting for whatever looked easiest. The street answered the way a street does. Old Hrenna stayed on her upturned bucket at the brazier and held her hands out to the coals. She is seventy, Sarkorian, and was carried off from a steading as a girl and kept in this city by the demons who held it, and she has been free for a few weeks. There is not very much left of her. She watched the whole thing with enormous patient eyes, and she did not move once, and if one of them had come at her it would have had her, and she knew it. Wat Crake selected a length of iron from among his wares and stood slapping it into his palm, waiting for anything at all to come onto his carpet. He was not frightened. He was annoyed. Piet Harl , a clerk in a black coat gone shiny at the elbows who kept books for a counting-house in Kenabres until Kenabres was opened, discovered that a clerk's velvet slippers are not running shoes and went down the street screaming, trailing paper out of his satchel. Dun Ferrow , a big slow porter, put down a sack of meal and spent a good part of the crisis working out whether he still got paid if he ran. Liesl Ambry , whose husband did not come back from the taking, got her body between Benno and the street and did not move again. The four strangers did something else. Riven stepped back and put a fist of blue-white light into the nearest centipede, a working that slid off his hand and kept the shape of it as it flew. The thing rolled up onto its front legs and came down again with a noise like heavy rain on a roof, and did not change its mind about where it was going. He shouted at Aldo Fenn to run, and Aldo ran, instantly and without arguing. Aldo is seventeen. He joined Iomedae's Preservers the week Drezen fell, because the Preservers are Harlock Greyforge's and Harlock Greyforge is the greatest man he has ever seen from a distance, and he has been drilled with a spear ever since by other recruits who do not know how either. Nobody had ever given him an order in a real fight before. He has thought about it a good deal since. The first magic in this chronicle, and it did not change the centipede's mind. Pip went under her father's handcart. The centipede went under after her. Dorogh got down on his belly in the filth of the street and worked a long spear into the gap, trying to reach the thing that was eating a child without putting the point through the child. It was not a clean angle and it did not give him a clean result. Kora put a crossbow bolt into a second centipede from across the way. Diddle, who had come to the market to buy herbs, decided that children being eaten was worth the risk, moved up along the stalls, and lobbed a vial that he thumbed open in the air. It burst and reduced a centipede to legs and flame. It also set fire to a fruit stall, which he went and stamped out afterwards. The centipede under the cart bit Pip through the middle and she stopped moving. Kora crossed twenty feet of contested street to get within reach of her, crouched to see under the axle, and made a sound like a chirp. There was a light so faint you had to already be looking for it to see it at midday. The girl's bleeding stopped. Her father, Oskar Venn , saw the wounds go still and took it for the worst news of his life. A light so faint you had to already be looking for it. Dorogh killed the thing under the cart from his belly and got up. Riven killed the one Kora had wounded. Hobb Garrow , who soldiered in the Fourth Crusade and has cooked for the Preservers' barracks ever since, was in the street arguing over a string of onions. He came in with his cleaver, took a bite and a dose of paralytic for his trouble, and stayed upright out of sheer indignation. The last centipede bit Jory Tallow as he tried to run from the crate he had opened, which he would later find theologically confusing. Dorogh put the spear through it, and the fight was over in less time than it takes to describe. Hobb then tackled Jory into the cobbles, sat on him, and shouted for the watch. Riven walked over and knocked the boy unconscious with one punch. It was a decisive blow, and everyone present agreed that it had looked easy. Diddle Scribes putting out the part of it that was his own fault. A Loaf of Bread a Day Diddle finished stamping out his own fire, crossed the street, tugged on Kora's cloak, and held a dull red vial up over his head. She had bandaged what could be bandaged and had reached the end of what bandages do. She looked at the goblin, and then at the scorch marks he had made and then put out, and decided that was as good a character reference as anyone gets. She poured the potion into the wound. It came up in a red foam and the skin closed smooth under it, and Pip pushed herself onto her elbows, reached up, and touched Kora's beak, and did not say anything at all. Oskar wanted their names. Diddle. Kora. Doro, said Dorogh, happy to help. \"I'm never going to forget you,\" Oskar told them. \"Anytime you want bread, it's on the house. A loaf a day for the rest of your lives.\" It is worth knowing what he was offering. Oskar Venn came up from Nerosyan behind the supply wagons a fortnight after the city fell, a widower with a child and a trade an army needs. He rents a cold oven in an empty house two streets over. He is a narrow, stooped man who apologises to large people before they have said anything, and the one thing in the world he is proud of is that his is the first bread baked in Drezen in seventy years. Riven, who had spent the fight in the open and the aftermath under a hood, went to help put out the last of the flames and gave his name to nobody in a uniform. The watch arrived from both ends of the street and converged on the one person in the market who had been paying complete attention. Bruna Aske sells pots and pans and ladles off a rug, and keeps the only honest scales in Lupenor's Market, which is a thing she has had a row with every trader on the street to establish. She had spent the entire incident standing on that rug holding her brass scales to her chest with both hands, declining to move off it for anybody, watching. Her account was accurate down to the antenna that had landed in her good silverware. She gave the watch the goblin's bomb and the goblin putting the fire out, both. She reported that the bird woman had cast something she could not name, which seemed to have worked, because the child was up and running. She is not on anybody's side. She is on the side of the scales. The watch asked where the cultist had gone. The garrison cook, Bruna said, meaning Hobb, had carried him off over his shoulder to Mira's . The watchmen nodded as though that settled the matter and went that way. A few minutes later somebody started calling silk again. Riven picked up the wax locust, which nobody had thought to collect. It was beeswax, scented for lavender, with thumbprints still in it where the boy had pressed and pulled and twisted it into shape. It was an ordinary lump of wax made by hand by somebody who was trying. They followed Marit to the candle shop, where a short round man with a walrus moustache heard what his apprentice had done, said the boy had not been right since Kenabres, and shut up shop to go and speak for him. He had noticed Jory slipping off somewhere the last few days. He did not pay him enough to go drinking, and the boy had not come back drunk, so he had left it alone. Nobody bought a candle. Two Pints and a Song They went for a drink instead, at a middling tavern called the Two Pints and a Song , and introduced themselves properly for the first time: a half-orc in padded armour and plain wire spectacles, apologising for his size before anybody complained of it; a goblin who took his hood off once he was warm; a tengu with a raven's head and talons under a light grey robe, who watches more than she speaks; and a tiefling with ram's horns and a staff he never quite set down. Somebody observed that they were a diverse bunch. Somebody else had fetched a bar stool over from the counter so that Diddle could sit at the table rather than under it. Nobody made anything of it. Diddle lives here. He came up with his master and does not regard Drezen as his natural environment, which explains a good deal about him. Kora is from the other side of the world and keeps a small fortune-telling shop on the edge of the city, where most of the work is legitimate. Riven travels from place to place looking for libraries. Kora's drink tipped over into her lap. Her hands were nowhere near it. She did not look surprised. She started cleaning it up. Riven, who had spent the morning throwing invisible fists at centipedes, filed the moment away and said nothing. Dorogh offered to buy her another. A half-orc, a goblin, a tengu, and a tiefling walk into a bar... There is no public library in Drezen. There are said to be whole rooms of books inside the citadel, available to privileged individuals. Then a boy of about fourteen came in at the door, looked the room over, decided, and walked to their table. \"The nightingale enters through the side door,\" he said, and looked from face to face, hoping somebody knew what that meant. \"The blade is locked,\" said Riven. \"Right this way.\" The Wall of Red String The side door was in an alley off the market, and the peephole was at eye level, and the woman behind it wanted a password. \"Password,\" said Riven. \"That's a horrible password,\" she said, and let him in. The room behind the door had a wall of paper in it. Floor plans. Scraps of parchment and cloth. Cramped writing that ran margin to margin as though it were the last paper in the world. Long looping hands. Some of it in code. All of it strung together with red wool running in every direction. The notes had not come out of one person's desk. One page among them was a diary entry written by a little girl. It was not signed, and it was not in Mira's hand. Jory Tallow was tied to a chair in the middle of it with one black eye and a bandaged bite. Mira Thistledance keeps Lupenor Celest's market while Lupenor rides west. She is a half-elf with green eyes and brown hair threaded with fine silver chains, a lute swinging behind her on its strap and a wax tablet in one hand covered edge to edge in cramped notes and arrows, and she has decided, with total delight, that her real position is spymaster of Drezen. She had watched the whole thing in the market from a second-floor window. She had already interrogated the boy. What she wanted now was to watch the four of them work. So Riven pulled his hood back, put his horns in the boy's entire field of vision, and observed that the only reliable way to purify a thing touched by demons is to set it on fire. The colour went out of Jory's face. Kora put a hand on his shoulder and told him to be calm and say what happened. Dorogh folded his arms and cracked his knuckles and did his best to look like hired muscle. It worked, in the sense that the boy said everything he knew in one breath. He had wanted to be on the winning side. He had been in Kenabres in Arodus when the city was opened, and had seen the thing in the sky above it, and had watched the crusade fail to stop it. He was certain Deskari would win and would crush anyone who was not his, so he had gone looking for people who worshipped Deskari, following rumours down to a scrapyard on the South Bank where cultists were said to gather. They were cultists. They served some other demon lord entirely. They laughed at him and sent him home. So he had gone down into the cellars of the abandoned houses on the South Bank alone, and caught giant centipedes, and kept them in a crate, and fed them rats until he had enough of them to stage an exhibition in the marketplace and get his god's attention. Riven remarked that being pious had never saved anybody's parents that he knew of. \"At least you had parents,\" Jory said. \"My father's face was dissolved by a giant fly. I watched it happen.\" Somebody pointed out that he had very nearly killed a seven-year-old girl. \"Deskari spared her, obviously,\" said Jory. \"I don't know why the centipedes attacked me, though.\" Mira did not want to hand him to Iomedae's Preservers , on the grounds that they would hang him, and she did not care for packing him off to be some other town's problem either. The suggestion that came out of the room was Sarenrae , who is a goddess of redemption, who keeps a church in the city, and who might conceivably take an apprentice. Mira shrugged and said she would ask, and that she hoped they would take him, because she hated to have to put him down. Then she rang a bell, and a bald man of about fifty who had spent most of those summers carrying heavy things came in, picked up the chair with the boy still tied to it, and carried both out the way he had come. \"I have plans for that boy,\" Mira said. \"Maybe we can use him as a double agent.\" Then she pulled a wax tablet off her desk. \"But first. Code names.\" Operatives in the ballads are birds, so Diddle was to be Wren, on the grounds that wrens are very loud and very small. Somebody observed that bird names might land badly with a tengu in the room. Mira conceded the point instantly, flipped the tablet over, and read off the alternates from the back. Diddle was Tomato, because he throws things. Dorogh was Yam. Kora was Carrot. Riven was Cabbage. Diddle asked whether he could be Pickle instead. He was not Pickle. Operative Cabbage, Operative Carrot, Operative Tomato and Operative Yam. The pitch followed. She is the spymaster of Drezen, she said, dropping her voice to a volume that carried, and she has been looking a long time for an elite strike team of master infiltrators, and this morning in the marketplace she found one. She had done a little checking. They were unaffiliated. They had no connection to her or to the city. They were completely deniable. \"You mean expendable.\" \"I hadn't thought of it that way.\" What was in it for them, Diddle asked. \"Glory. And a steady paycheck.\" \"How is it glory if it's undercover?\" \"It's very quiet glory. Few people will know. Very well-connected people.\" She brightened. \"And eventually you'll all be dead, and they'll sing songs about you.\" There is a cult embedded in the South Bank, at a scrapyard on Cinder Row , where people go in and do not always come out, and where sometimes more come out than went in. Mira wanted a head count, the name of the demon lord they serve, and their plans, and she would pay a hundred and fifty gold for it, more for better intelligence. Her operational proposal was that the company infiltrate the place disguised as a troupe of acrobats. When that landed poorly she offered monks. She was also still hunting for the right name for the strike force. She tried the Quiet Knives on them, and made the whole room say it with her, and did not seem to love it either. She offered the Market Shadows. She offered Operation Nightingale, and then took it straight back: that was a mission name, she said, and she was saving it for a mission worth the having. Nobody told her. Somebody countered with the Marketplace Irregulars. It was agreed that the question could wait. They signed on for the one job, as a trial. Riven asked for library access as part of the terms, and Mira thought research was a wonderful idea and tore a sheet of parchment in half and wrote him a note on it. Then they worked out a signal system, and she took it down on the wax tablet as they went. A red flag tied to the water spout in the market meant she needed to talk. A blue flag meant they did. A purple flag meant she needed to talk, purple being nearly red. Any other flag on the water spout meant that somebody had tied a flag to the water spout. Somebody asked what had become of her last team. \"There hasn't been a last team. This is my first strike team.\" She beamed at them. \"You're doing great, by the way.\" The Bearer Is Singular The note worked, in the narrowest possible sense. The citadel gates were shut for the night and six guards were on them, and they watched a hooded tiefling with a stick come up the causeway after dark, followed by an upright black bird, a goblin, and a half-orc. The guard who stepped forward to take the note was either the most senior or the least fortunate. He flipped it open, looked at it, and turned to the others. \"Anybody here read?\" One of them could. The note entitled the bearer to full access to the library at the citadel and was signed Mira Thistledance, and the guards took the view that this meant precisely one person. An argument followed. The company put it to the gate that they were one unit, and that a unit holds a note collectively. The literate guard observed that bearer is singular and not plural. The others noted that the four of them were not of a height, were not of a race, and did not have the same number of fingers. A man was sent in to ask a sergeant. The sergeant said no. Kora threw a voice behind them suggesting that somebody in authority had cleared it, and the guards agreed that a voice had spoken and did not believe a word of it. Diddle went home. His master was still waiting on the morning's ingredients, and Diddle has outlived the average goblin by a comfortable margin and intends to go on doing it. So Riven went in alone, and Kora walked back across the city in the dark and knocked on Mira's side door, and Mira opened the peephole and said, \"Agent Carrot. What are you doing here?\" and wrote out two more notes without asking a single question, because research is wonderful. The library held four or five hundred books, none of them light and none of them recent. They were the sort of thing a long-dead wizard writes after visiting somewhere he should not have gone, and they were no use whatsoever for finding out who is in your city this week. For a demon lord they were excellent. Deskari is the Lord of the Locust Host. The Worldwound is not simply a hole in the world. The far side of it opens into his realm, so that a man may walk out of Golarion into Deskari's country without troubling the Abyss in between, and much of what lives in the Worldwound belongs to him. Madness is among his domains, and very few humans worship him on purpose. Those who do are generally a little crazy, unless they are a lot crazy. \"So we're dealing with crazy people,\" said somebody. \"We already were,\" said somebody else. After Deskari, the second most written-about demon lord on those shelves was Baphomet , which said something about whoever had assembled the collection. Dorogh spent an hour on the question of whether the two were rivals, which would have been useful, and established instead that they are allies, which was not. He came away with Baphomet's holy symbols: a pentacle with a goat's head at the centre, two backward curves meeting like a letter, and a labyrinth. An older woman at the next table told them to keep it down. She was studying. What she was studying was none of their concern. They went home to sleep on it, and agreed to meet at the alchemist's shop after breakfast. A Copper a Pound They met at the alchemist's shop after breakfast, where Diddle's master opened up to find three strangers on his step asking for his goblin, and elected not to ask about it. Dorogh greeted Diddle as Pickle. Diddle corrected him: Tomato. Operative Tomato. Dorogh observed that Pickle was what he had asked for. It was settled that he could have both, on the grounds that Mira was not going to check. The South Bank is the worst part of Drezen and nobody in Drezen argues the point. It is where the slaves were housed during the occupation. It has no reliable water, no arable ground, no decent roads, no street lamps, no lamp oil, no heating oil, and very little watch. Nobody sleeps rough down there, because there are more abandoned houses than there are people to fill them and some of the roofs are still mostly on. Rubbish goes at the side of the road. The Ahari has been dry for months, so you cross it on a bridge over nothing. The 27th of Neth was cold enough to see your breath. The scrapyard on Cinder Row was a fair-sized house with a yard behind it, walled in an eight-foot fence of uneven planks. There was a red arrow slapped onto the boards pointing round into the alley, a sandwich board that said SCRAP, and a sign on the gate that said BEWARE OF DOGS. There were two, and they were the one thing in that yard that could have been settled kindly. Riven found the first of them by its nose in a knothole and, seeing no better idea on offer, reached through the fence with a spectral hand and rubbed its belly. The dog lost its mind. The second dog joined in. Between the yelping and the chain-rattling the company established that the yard held two large animals of the same line, one younger and the other missing half an ear and an entire leg, and that they were not so much fighting as disagreeing. Through the gaps in the planks they also established a pile of salvaged lumber, a pile of masonry sorted by colour, and a heap of rust. Dorogh knocked at the office door as an honest man looking for work. The man who opened it had a scrap hook in one hand and a hangover behind his eyes, and quoted a copper a pound for good steel, with everything under that open to negotiation. They took lumber, stone, wagon parts, machine parts. Tinkerers came by looking to repair things. Pound on the back gate when you come, he said, and he would chain the dogs and weigh it out. It was a scrapyard. It was an entirely functional, unglamorous scrapyard. Inked on the inside of the man's wrist was a labyrinth. Kora saw it, and said nothing at all until they were well down the alley. They talked it over somewhere private. Two men at least, sleeping where they worked, which made a night approach worse rather than better. Two dogs. A tattoo. The word of a half-mad boy who had been turned away at that gate. Dorogh, who knows the difference between a load-bearing wall and salvageable lumber, spent an hour building a sled out of a door and two lengths of mismatched rope, while the others chipped sixty pounds of ceramic tile off the wall of what had been a bathhouse and gathered a hundred pounds of short planks. It looked like an honest haul because it was one. The plan they agreed on was to go in as customers and take the yard without killing anybody, on the grounds that the city watch takes a keen interest in murder and none whatsoever in a scrapyard. The dogs heard the sled coming from thirty feet out. The company heard somebody inside kick one of them quiet. The gate opened on a worker complaining that it had only been an hour and he had barely slept, and then giving up and waving them in. He set a scale up by the wall and began turning over the tile with his hook. The second man went to sit with the dogs. Both of them had the same labyrinth on them, one on the upper arm and one on the back of the wrist. Diddle admired the tattoo out loud. The man squinted at him, said \"What of it,\" and went back to testing the chains. Diddle then asked when the scale had last been calibrated, and the man offered to wait while he went home for his own weights, and spat on the ground. Kora drifted along the piles, browsing, until she could see through the door into the house: a table, a hearth, a pair of chairs, and two more men eating breakfast. Riven found a heap big enough to crouch behind and put an armour of force around himself where nobody could see him do it. Then Dorogh, with two men in front of him and no way to ask the others whether the moment had come, decided that it had, and bolted through the door. He came through it at a dead run behind the shield, roaring, and swung it at the head of a man who had his back turned and was expecting nothing at all, and missed, and spilled his coffee. Operative Yam springs the ambush. After that it went the way these things go. Diddle stepped back, judged the distance, and put a bomb into the worker by the kennel, which dropped him at once, set fire to the lumber pile he had been standing on, and singed both dogs, who began to scream in their chains. The man at the scale finally looked up from his weights to find an explosion behind him, a woman shrieking in the house, and his customers gone. He came at Dorogh with the hook and missed. A woman came out of a side room, heavily pregnant, asking what all this was. Riven laid a working of exhaustion on her and she sagged back into the doorway and held it. Her husband is Hesk Dolvan , a scrap dealer past fifty in a good coat gone shiny at the elbows, with a scrap merchant's hands and a scrap merchant's habit of pricing whatever is in front of him before he decides about it. He stood up from the breakfast table, took a hatchet off his belt that was more tool than weapon, said \"Stay away from my wife,\" and put it into Riven, who went down in the doorway and stayed down. Somewhere close by, a baby started screaming. Something thumped against the door Alia Dolvan was holding shut, and the handle rattled, and she did not let it open. Kora put a crossbow bolt into the man with the black chin whiskers. Diddle went past everybody's legs at a dead run, announcing a medic coming through, knelt in the doorway, and poured his last potion into Riven, who came back to himself face down on the floor of a stranger's house. It was the second he had spent in two days and the last one he had. Kora got in beside Dorogh and closed his wounds while a hatchet was being swung at the pair of them. Dorogh killed the man he had failed to knock out. The worker with the bolts in his back gave up on the yard, ran inside past the company, and began fumbling at a trapdoor set into the middle of the floor. It was chained shut. He did not have the key. He went out the far door instead and kept going. Alia got past Riven while he was too hurt to stop her, went into her own kitchen, found a knife where she knew it would be, and began working up the courage to use it on him. A door opened. Sera Dolvan , who is twelve, looked out at the burning yard, at the man lying face down across the table, at the half-orc with the morningstar and the goblin at his feet, and then closed the door again. Behind it were her brother Corin , who is nine and was shouting for his mother, and the baby in its crib. The dogs were still in their chains and the lumber was still burning, and nobody had yet said the word Baphomet out loud inside that house. The company had come to count cultists. What they had found so far was a family, a trapdoor that nobody was allowed to open, and a fight they had started themselves. Session of September 19, 2026."}];
window.SECRETS = [{"id":"sec1","order":1,"title":"Where You Come From: A Character Bio Helper","subtitle":"The Real Work. The lands around Drezen, for anyone writing a character who is standing in the city in the autumn of 4713 AR and has not yet worked out why.","category":"History and Accounts","catOrder":3,"md":"# Where You Come From: A Character Bio Helper\n\n*The Real Work. The lands around Drezen, for anyone writing a character who is standing in the city in the autumn of 4713 AR and has not yet worked out why.*\n\n<!-- toc -->\n\nThis is a handout for building a backstory. It is not a chapter, not a rulebook, and not a\npromise that any of it will still be true by Kuthona. It gathers what passes for fact about the\ncountry around **Drezen** (the Worldwound, old Sarkoris, Mendev and its capital Nerosyan) from\nPaizo's published setting and from the crusade's own chronicle at\n[The Fifth Crusade](https://mattdahse.github.io/the-fifth-crusade/), and it stops at the present\nday.\n\n**Which source wins.** Where Paizo and the chronicle differ, the chronicle governs at this table.\nWhere the chronicle is silent, Paizo's setting stands. Where both are silent, make something up\nand tell the DM, so that it can be written down and later contradicted by a dice roll.\n\n**How to use it.** Skim [§1](#h-1-the-moment-you-are-standing-in) so you know what everyone in the city knows. Read the one or two\nsections that match where your character is from. Then see [§8](#h-8-building-the-bio). The map of\nDrezen is on this site's [Map tab](#/map/drezen), in the same voice as this document.\n\n**The general situation, for those in a hurry.** A god was due back in 4606 to settle everything.\nHe died instead. A hole opened where he was supposed to stand, and everything since has been\npeople building walls in front of the hole and demons taking the walls down, for a hundred and\nseven years, with great sincerity on both sides. The demons are enjoying it more. You have been\nhired to do the part of this that does not fit in a song.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The moment you are standing in\n\nIt is **Neth, 4713 AR**. Late autumn, with the first hard frosts on the Marchlands and the\nsouthern horizon lit orange after dark. Everyone in Drezen carries these facts, mostly the way\none carries a bad tooth.\n\n**Kenabres fell on Armasse, the 16th of Arodus.** Iomedae's own feast day. The Storm King came\nover the walls, the silver dragon **Terendelev** died in the sky above the city, the great bridge\nwent down, and the wardstone in the Kite was shattered. The crusade's northern wall for a century\nfell in an afternoon. Prelate **Hulrun** had spent fifty years burning people to keep the demons\nout. The demons killed him first.\n\n**The wardstones are gone.** A week later, at the **Gray Garrison**, four strangers out of the\nrubble broke the corrupted fragment before the enemy could turn it against the army gathering at\nthe border, and every wardstone along the West Sellen went dark with it. The border that held for\nseventy-five years is now a river. The power the stones held went into the four. The church has\nruled this a miracle. The Riftwardens have asked to see the paperwork.\n\n**The four** are **Rabiah**, a sorcerer of sixteen; **Harlock Greyforge**, a half-orc paladin\nwho carries a sword that talks; **Lupenor Celest**, an elven archer; and **Varic Sarian**, a\npriest of Sarenrae. Mendev calls them the Wardstone's Champions, and there is a ballad. You will\nhear it in every tavern between here and Egede, sung by people who could not find Drezen on a\nchart, and the verse about the forty at the door gets longer every month. The four are away in\nthe west now, days out of contact. They do not otherwise come into this document. You will not be\nworking for them. You will be working for the people they left behind, who are less famous and\npay in coin.\n\n**Queen Galfrey rode north** to Kenabres herself, and sent the champions to Drezen with a hundred\nvolunteers. They marched out on the 30th of Arodus, took **Vilareth Ford** back on the road,\ndeclined to walk into **Keeper's Canyon** in the dark, and came within sight of Drezen's walls on\nthe 6th of Rova. Most of the hundred are alive. Nobody had planned for that, and the barracks are\nshort of beds.\n\n**Drezen changed hands on Fire Day, the 13th of Rova, 4713.** The citadel that fell to the demons\nin 4638 is a crusader city again. The ***Sword of Valor***, the banner lost when it fell, was\ncarried out of the dungeons beneath it, flawless after seventy-five years in demon hands.\n**Staunton Vhane**, the dwarf who betrayed the city three generations ago, is buried here. The\nbanner came through the occupation without a stain. Nothing else did.\n\n**Sir Aldrich Thorne** came from the Queen the next day, by teleportation, with two guards. He\nsaw the city passed to **Irabeth Tirabade** to govern, declined a meal, and left the same\nafternoon. He has not been back. Ask anyone in Drezen what the Queen's man thought of the place\nand they will tell you how long he stayed.\n\n**The crusade holds the walls and the citadel.** It does not yet hold every quarter. The people\nholding the rest are hiring. You are what they hired.\n\nIf your character has been in Drezen more than a week, they have also heard: that the demons\nthemselves have posted a thousand platinum for a succubus somewhere in the west, more than the\ncrusade has ever offered for anything; that a shrine of Sarenrae two days down the western road\nhas been reopened and is kept by a priest named **Rothin Vald**; that the **South Bank** has not\nbeen searched; and that **Horgus Gwerm**, the quartermaster, is browbeating the crown for grain.\nHis last dispatch is public knowledge because he read it aloud in the market first:\n\n> *Your Majesty. Drezen holds. Drezen has four hundred mouths more than it had on the 13th and\n> the same grain. I am told the crown's stores are committed to Kenabres. I am told a great many\n> things. Send wheat, or send fewer people, or send Sir Aldrich back to explain it to them; he\n> will not need long.*\n>\n> *Gwerm, Quartermaster.*\n\n### The dates that matter\n\n| Year (AR) | Event | Declared | What it did |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 3672 | Sarkoris founded when the Kellid clans broke the frost giants of the Northmounds. | A kingdom. | Built a kingdom on top of the barrows. |\n| 4433 | Aroden drives the cult of Deskari out of the Northmounds into the Lake of Mists and Veils. | The end of the cult. | Moved the cult. |\n| 4601 | The last prince of Mendev dies on an embassy to Sarkoris. His daughter **Galfrey** is crowned. | Mysterious. | Still mysterious. |\n| 4606 | Aroden, prophesied to return, dies. The Worldwound opens. **Iz** falls first. | The end of the world. | Just the north. |\n| 4622–4630 | **First Crusade.** Demons pushed back to the Northmounds. **Drezen** founded. Nerosyan walled. | A success. | Told the demons how the crusade fights. |\n| 4638 | **Drezen falls** to the marilith **Aponavisius**, betrayed from within by Staunton Vhane, a paladin. | A tragedy. | Also that. |\n| 4638–4645 | **Second Crusade.** Sarkoris written off. The ***wardstones*** raised; the first in the Kite at Kenabres, 4639. | A failure. | Held the line for seventy-five years. |\n| 4665–4668 | **Third Crusade.** Demonic infiltration answered with witch hunts. Hundreds burned at Kenabres. | A purification. | Burned Mendevians. Ran out of Mendevians. Stopped. |\n| c. 4670 | Gundrun, in the far south-west, rebuilt by Sarkorian survivors. | Nothing; nobody noticed. | The only free town in the Wound. |\n| 4684 | The ***Demonscope*** placed at Clydwell Keep. | Vigilance. | A very good view of the demons. |\n| 4692–4707 | **Fourth Crusade.** The balor **Khorramzadeh**, the Storm King, cracks the Kenabres wardstone. Fifteen years. The **Order of Heralds** ends the purges. | A stalemate. | Everyone went home tired. The Heralds got this cell in a table. |\n| 4713, 16 Arodus | **Kenabres falls.** | The end of the world. | See 4606. |\n| 4713, 23 Arodus | The last wardstone fragment destroyed by its own defenders. | Heresy, for about a day. | It worked. |\n| 4713, 13 Rova | **Drezen retaken.** | A turning point. | Four hundred extra mouths and the same grain. |\n| 4713, Neth | *Now.* | | |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mendev: the crusader state\n\n**The land.** The northern end of the continent: snow-covered mountains, icy steppe, and the\ngreat evergreen **Estrovian Forest** across the centre and south. North, the ice cliffs of the\nCrown of the World. East, the **Lake of Mists and Veils**, which every crusader crossed to get\nhere. South, across the rivers, **Numeria**, a harsh country of savages and alien metal that\nMendevians want nothing to do with. West, the **West Sellen River**, and on its far bank the\nWorldwound. There is almost no arable land. For a century the crusade has been fed by boats,\ncrusaders and grain coming up the Sellen and across the lake, arriving weekly at **Egede**. The\ngrain is inspected. The crusaders are not.\n\n**Three kinds of Mendevian.** Nobody in Mendev is just \"a Mendevian.\" Ask which.\n\n- **The natives.** Descended from Iobarian exiles, semi-nomadic once, and cousins to the\n  Sarkorians across the river. Many still keep the old **animist** faith or the **Green Faith**,\n  quietly. The crusaders have treated them as second-class for a hundred years and in the Third\n  Crusade burned them for heresy. A native Mendevian character has a grandmother who remembers\n  who informed on whom. The crown kept no such list. The grandmothers did.\n- **The crusaders and their children.** From Cheliax, Isger, Andoran, Taldor and everywhere\n  else. The first wave were the finest people of the south, clerics and paladins and knights who\n  sold their lands to come. By the Fourth Crusade the boats were bringing mercenaries,\n  dispossessed nobles, political undesirables and men let out of prison on condition they went\n  north, and these now outnumber the pious. A crusader's-child character can be third-generation\n  Mendevian and still be a foreigner to the natives, whose great-uncle their grandfather may have\n  denounced.\n- **The Sarkorians.** Kellid refugees of the lost kingdom, three generations in exile. See [§5](#h-5-old-sarkoris-the-country-that-was).\n  In Nerosyan they have a quarter of their own along the western wall, the **Fire Circle**.\n  Others took the caves of **Saresp Mountain** in the far east, or scattered into Numeria,\n  Ustalav and beyond.\n\nAnd among them: **tieflings** in unusual numbers (Mendev is home to many asura-spawn, and the\nWound breeds pitborn); **dwarves** whose ancestors fled the lost Sky Citadel of **Jormurdun**\nwith a royal claim and a piece of a broken key, and who mostly live poor in the Fleshward slum of\nChesed, still holding the piece; halflings; half-elves; and beneath Kenabres, the **mongrelmen**,\nthe warped descendants of First Crusade soldiers whom the Wound changed and whom their own\ncomrades then tried to kill.\n\n**Rule.** A monarchy under **Queen Galfrey**, the Sword of Iomedae, a Taldan woman who looks to\nbe in her middle forties and is over a hundred years old, held there by the sun orchid elixir.\nCrowned in 4601 as a paladin of Aroden; five years later Aroden died, and she retrained herself\nas Iomedae's paladin with the brisk practicality of a woman whose first god had let her down. She\nordered the retreat from Sarkoris in 4638 so the wardstones could be raised, and has been hated\nfor it since by the descendants of the people it kept alive. Her word is law within Nerosyan's\nwalls. Beyond them, every commander of every city, fort and army interprets her commands a little\ndifferently. Until this summer the second most powerful person in the kingdom was **Prelate\nHulrun Shappok** of Kenabres, whose witch-burnings as a young man started the Third Crusade, and\nwho only in his last years began to understand the shame he had brought on the faith. He was\nworking on it.\n\n**Faith.** **Iomedae**, the Inheritor, is the crusade. Her white-and-gold banner is the one all\ncrusaders nominally fight under, her cathedral is the centre of Nerosyan, and her holy day\n**Armasse** is the day Kenabres fell. She inherited her portfolio from Aroden, who died on the\nday he was due to return. The Chapel of the Argent Shield on the Egede road, once his, still\nkeeps a chair for him by the altar. Nobody sits in it. Nobody has moved it either. Beside her:\n**Sarenrae**, whose priests march with the columns and heal what the columns leave; **Shelyn**,\n**Desna**, **Erastil**, **Abadar**, **Pharasma**; **Gorum** in the barracks of Egede; **Cayden\nCailean**, whose neighbourhood in Nerosyan has the city's best-attended services. The Green Faith\nand the animist ways of the natives survive, and are looked at sideways. Every Mendevian, pious\nor not, knows the gesture: fingers to the lips, then pressed to the sword-pommel. Ask a Mendevian\nwhat it does. Watch them do it again while they think.\n\nThree sayings from any Mendevian kitchen:\n\n> *Aroden is coming. Set a place.*\\\n> *The wall is only as good as the priest is sober.*\\\n> *Pray to Iomedae. Pay Abadar. Drink with Cayden. Bury with Pharasma. Ask the trees when nobody's looking.*\n\n**Orders and outfits.** A crusader belongs to something, or pretends to.\n\n- **The Eagle Watch**: the crusaders of Kenabres. **Irabeth Tirabade** commanded them at the\n  Defender's Heart; **Anevia**, her wife, served among them.\n- **The Order of Heralds**: curbed the Third Crusade's purges and turned the war back outward.\n  Still the conscience of the crusade. See the table above for their reward.\n- **The Riftwardens**: a secretive society of planar scholars who want the Worldwound *closed*,\n  not merely held, based in Kenabres's **Librarium of the Broken Black Wing**, which has since\n  partly collapsed. **Aravashnial**, an elven wizard blinded in the fall of Kenabres and since\n  healed by the Queen's own priests, is one. Their mark is the Seeker's Spiral. Do not give it to\n  a new character without asking.\n- **Iomedae's Preservers**: the newest of the orders, founded this autumn by **Harlock\n  Greyforge** and headquartered in Drezen. Five squads sworn to hold the city. Their recruiting\n  bill is nailed to the citadel gate:\n  > *IOMEDAE'S PRESERVERS seek the steadfast. Three days' trial. Bring your own boots. Food\n  > provided on the third day. Those who leave before the third day are asked to return the\n  > boots if they were not their own. Apply to Dame Elara Dawnstrider, who will know if you are\n  > lying.*\n- **The Kenabres Wolves**: the spies and scouts, **Aron Kir**'s people. He is Warden of Drezen\n  now.\n- **The Order of Holy Sentinels**: guardians of the ***Demonscope*** at **Clydwell Keep**, the\n  greatest of the border forts. Every fort on the old wardstone line is named for a dead general.\n- **The knightly orders**: the Emerald Sword, the Flaming Lance, the Rampant Dragon, the Sunrise\n  Sword, the Guild of Diggers and Sappers, and dozens more. Some are ancient. Some were invented\n  on the boat, because the heralds of Iomedae cannot check every newcomer's claim to a title.\n  The heralds know this. The knights know the heralds know. A title is a lie everyone has agreed\n  on, and Mendev has agreed on a great many.\n- **The Low Templars**: the crusade coined the name for its mercenaries and sellswords, then\n  noticed it had coined a name for most of the crusade, and kept it.\n- **The Pathfinder Society**: in Nerosyan since the Queen gave them the Starrise Spire in 4707,\n  sending expeditions into the Wound disguised as mercenary companies.\n- **The inquisitors**: Hulrun's people. Fewer than they were, and quieter since Kenabres.\n\n**Banners.** Any crusader stronghold is a riot of colour, flags from hundreds of clans, counties,\nkingdoms and families competing for room. The banners that matter are earned on the field and\ntake the form of Iomedae's own symbols, the **sword**, the **shield** or the **sunburst**, granted\nto soldiers of any rank. It is the one honour in Mendev that cannot be bought, and it is a piece\nof cloth.\n\n**Speech.** Mendevians speak **Taldane** (Common); the natives and the Sarkorians also speak\n**Hallit**, the Kellid tongue, which has no written form. A **gaffle** or a **rip** is a scam.\nThere is no Mendevian word for an honest bargain. They use the Taldane, carefully.\n\n**The wardstones, and their absence.** For seventy-five years the border was a line of\nrune-carved menhirs along the West Sellen and the Moutray, fed with **nexovar** from the hills of\nthe Egelsee, maintained by daily prayer and a yearly ritual, and housed in a chain of forts.\nNothing chaotic or evil could pass between them, and no teleportation could cross the line. They\nare gone since Arodus. The forts are still garrisoned. The prayers are still said every morning\nover stones that no longer hear them. A character who served on the line has lost the whole\nmeaning of their posting and kept the posting. The pay still comes.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Nerosyan: the Diamond of the North\n\nThe capital, and the crusade's fortress. Before the Worldwound it was a run-down river town of\npirates; the First Crusade widened its streets, walled it, and gave it a holy purpose, and the\npirates' grandchildren are now the city guard. About sixty-five thousand people, nearly all human.\n\n**Where it sits.** At the junction where the **Egelsee River** runs into the northernmost branch\nof the **West Sellen**. The Egelsee is the city's only clean water; the Sellen comes down too\nclose to the Wound to drink. Everything outside the walls has been burned flat, by demonic raids\nand by the defenders, so that nothing approaches unseen. Nobody now remembers which fires were\nwhose.\n\n**How it is built.** A diamond between the two rivers, so that every wall has water in front of\nit. The walls are twenty feet thick, and the wall-tops are *roofed*, sloped and cut with arrow\nslits, because the enemy flies. At the centre stands the **Cruciform Cathedral** of Iomedae, and\nthe avenues run wide and straight from it so that a sortie can reach any wall in minutes, and so\nthat the cathedral can see every door. Four towers hold the points: the **Battle Tower** in the\nnorth, the most heavily fortified structure in Mendev; the **Woundward Tower**, holding the\ncity's wardstone and its best archers; the **Confluence Tower**, guarding the rivers and\nCrusader's Quay; and the **Egelsee Tower**, the cavalry's ground. Above it all burns the\n**Starrise Spire**, older than the walls, with a ball of light floating over it that has not gone\nout in centuries: a beacon to the crusade and a warning to the Wound, which has had a century to\nget used to it.\n\n**The districts.** Battle, Egelsee, Confluence and Woundward are the four quarters. Within them:\n**Pilgrim's Rest**, where the endless stream of Iomedaean pilgrims lodges; **Bannerbright**,\nwhere the orders keep their houses and their feuds; the **Two Cs**, Cayden Cailean's\nneighbourhood, which is where the taverns are; the **Fire Circle** along the western wall, where\nthe Sarkorian refugees keep their shops and eating houses in the old style, facing the wall,\nfacing home. **Hank the Alerunner's** hideout is a tavern full of relics of the pirate days.\n**Ancuin's Brewery** has been the crusaders' favourite for a hundred years and is the institution\nwith the best record in Mendev.\n\nFrom the pamphlet handed to pilgrims at the Sellen Docks, fourth printing:\n\n> *WELCOME TO NEROSYAN, DIAMOND OF THE NORTH. Pilgrims will find lodging in Pilgrim's Rest, at\n> rates set by the crown. Pilgrims will find other lodging in the Two Cs, at rates set by the\n> Two Cs. The Cathedral is open at all hours. The Woundward Tower is not a viewing platform.\n> Please do not feed the Sarkorians; they have their own quarter and their own food and they\n> would like you to know that. Recruiters are stationed at every gate and are not permitted to\n> follow you into the Cathedral. They know this. Walk quickly.*\n\n**Who runs it.** The Queen, from her palace, counselled by an angel named **Opaline**, who has\npresumably seen the whole plan and is not sharing. Around her, **Lord Inquisitor Casori**,\n**Captain Jaspar** of the Crusader Heralds, and Venture-Captain **Jorsal of Lauterbury** of the\nPathfinders.\n\n**What has happened lately.** For years the city has been harried by things that come up the\nrivers, merrow and scrags and grindylows in the service of the hezrou that rules ruined Storasta\ndownriver, and there are stories of a larger siege this year, turned back with the Pathfinders in\nthe thick of it, which the Pathfinders tell. The wagons of conscripts and supply have been\nrolling north to Kenabres and on to Drezen since Rova. They leave Nerosyan with twelve conscripts\nand reach Drezen with nine. The quartermaster's ledger records this as *shrinkage*.\n\n**Bio hooks from Nerosyan.** A dock-hand who has unloaded a hundred boatloads of heroes and\nwatched none come back down. A cathedral acolyte who has never been outside the walls and\nbelieves everything, so far. A Two Cs brawler with a holy excuse. A child of the Fire Circle who\nspeaks Hallit at home and Taldane in the street and lies in both. A brewery drayman, the most\nrespected trade in the city. A conscript caught stealing bread and put on a supply wagon, who\nslipped away the moment it passed Drezen's gate. That is how **Tam**, a water-carrier in\nDrezen, arrived. Asked about it, Tam said, \"I was shrinkage.\"\n\n---\n\n## 4. Kenabres and the border: what fell, and what stands\n\n**Kenabres** was the crusade's northern city: twelve thousand people on tiers rising from the\neastern plain, its western edge a cliff above the West Sellen. **Old Kenabres** at the top held\nthe oldest families and the administration, which were the same families; the **Ring District**\nwas the rest of the original town; **New Kenabres** was built for refugees of the lost\nsettlements, and the **Gate District** ten years later when New Kenabres overflowed, because the\nsettlements kept getting lost. The **Truestone Quarry**, ten miles east, sent stone weekly to\nthe walls. The road to Egede is 225 miles and ten days by caravan, past the **Chapel of the\nArgent Shield**, with its chair.\n\nIt was also the crusade's conscience and its shame, which in Kenabres were the same building:\nthe seat of Hulrun's inquisition, where the generals **Dyre** and **Marcovina** fought the\nPrelate for every soldier he wanted kept home to hunt heretics instead of demons. Heretics were\neasier to find. The **Kite** held the first wardstone ever raised. The **Blackwing Library** held\nthe crusade's greatest store of learning on the demons and the planes. All of it was consulted.\n\n**What is left.** Half the population, about six and a half thousand, in a city split by rifts,\nits cathedral and the Kite destroyed, the library partly collapsed, and the Queen's own appointee\nin the Prelate's chair. The **Defender's Heart**, the fortified inn where Irabeth held the\nsurvivors together, is the heart of what remains. When the cathedral fell, the people went to the\npub, and the pub held. The chalkboard by its door has not been wiped since Arodus:\n\n> *BEDS: none. SOUP: yes. WATER: boil it. MISSING: ask Anevia. FOUND: ask Anevia.\n> MONGRELMEN ARE CUSTOMERS. Signed, I. Tirabade.*\n>\n> *(beneath, in another hand) she means it*\n\nThe assault tore open the tunnels under the city, and out of them came the mongrelmen of\n**Neathholm**, a hamlet of a few dozen on an island in an underground lake, under its one-eyed\nchief **Chusul**. They lived under the holiest city in the north for ninety years and the holiest\ncity never once looked down.\n\n**The road north.** **Valas's Gift**, a village of a couple of hundred north of Kenabres, named\nfor a holy man and its red-water springs. The springs were red before the war. The **Vineyard\nHills Waystation**, a day's ride south-east, a crossroads outpost well behind the old wardstone\nline, and attacked anyway. The **Estrovian Forest**, where few go willingly: hernes, stag-horned\nthings that were once trespassing huntsmen, cursed by druids who then died of their own curse;\nwitchwargs; a pack of owlbears that may be ghosts; taiga giants; and the werebear-kin, the only\nguides who will take you through, and the only inhabitants who could eat you and generally\nchoose not to. **Clydwell Keep** and the named forts, at full strength, guarding the memory of a\nline. And far off, the cursed ruins of **Icerift Castle**, about which people do not talk.\n\n**Egede**, on the lake. The second city, nearly forty thousand, the end of the River Road and the\nfirst Mendevian ground most crusaders set foot on. Where exhausted soldiers are sent to rest,\nwhich means prayer at the temples or the covert brothels. The temples know about the brothels\nand the brothels know about the temples and both keep the other's hours. Where merchants have\nmade an industry of bankrupting pilgrims on their way to the war, so that the demons get them\npre-stripped. Aroden drowned a demon lord here once, and Egedens celebrate **Drowning Day** for\ntwenty-four hours in a way outsiders find disturbing. They do not set a place for him. Egede has\nalways been practical.\n\n**Bio hooks from Kenabres.** Where were you on Armasse? In the Gate District when the bridge came\ndown. In the quarry caravan, ten miles out, watching the sky do something the sky should not. In\nthe cells of the inquisition, which turned out to be the safest place in the city. In the\ntunnels, and you did not know the tunnels were there. Among the refugees in the Defender's Heart\nwhen everyone decided, on no evidence, that things were about to get better. A city watchman\nwith twelve years' service and a burn scar you explain differently every time. A carpenter who\nlost workshop, tools and wife in an afternoon and has been very calm about it. A mongrelman of\nNeathholm above ground for the first time, finding it overrated.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Old Sarkoris: the country that was\n\nFor a thousand years before the Worldwound, the land west of the Sellen was **Sarkoris**: a\nsavage, proud, painted Kellid kingdom of fiercely independent clans, founded in 3672 when the\ntribes broke the frost giants of the Northmounds and took the Northmounds as a prize. Everything\non the crusade's charts west of the river is Sarkorian, because the crusade has never had\nanything better to put in its place. You can conquer a country three times and still be using its\nmaps.\n\n**The people.** **Kellids**: lean, dark-haired, light-brown-skinned, with dark eyes and scars\nthey are proud of. A Kellid without scars is thought to have lived too comfortably, and a Kellid\nwho has lived comfortably is asked at whose expense. They dress in leather, fur and hide, and\nwear **zoic fetishes**, charms of twine, hair and stone that their druids enchant. They value\nstrength, bravery and cunning; they have no patience for compromise or weak leaders; they carry\nonly what the clan needs; they respect Ulfen and dwarves and distrust magic and machines, having\nlived between Irrisen's witches and Numeria's engines and seen what each does to a neighbour.\nThe **clan** comes before the self. Names are short and percussive: **Dron**, **Gurog**,\n**Kronug**; **Belka**, **Inkit**, **Yala**. Second names are for the distinguished; a clan name\nmatters more than either. Names of record run longer and stranger: **Uloric Dziergas** the\nwarlord, **Domora Hume** the god-caller, **Khraigorr Half-Face**, who got the name honestly, the\n**Tarnshiavs** of Dyinglight, the **Widowknife** clanhold, the **Farheaven** clan.\n\n**The clans and their rulers.** Sarkoris was never one thing, which is why it took the demons\nalmost forty years to finish it. Each clan-hold had its **clanliege**; the great cities had their\n**witch-wardens**; warlords rose and fell. The mystics of Sarkoris warned, in the years before\n4606, of ascendant chaos and a thinning between the worlds. They were right about everything. No\none listened, because Aroden was coming, and a place had been set.\n\n**God-calling.** The heart of Sarkorian religion. A **god-caller** binds a spirit, the clan's own\npatron, usually a native beast made strange, a bear with feathers, a wolf with wings, and calls\nit into the world, where the clan worships it as their god. The god turns up. This is the whole of\nthe doctrine and the whole of the appeal. Southern churches call it the worship of false gods, on\nthe authority of true ones who have not been seen since 4606. Many of the clan-gods were\ndestroyed or driven off in the first years of the Wound. The demons could find them. A Sarkorian\nof god-calling blood may carry a name for a god no one has seen in a hundred years, and a grudge\non its behalf.\n\n**The old gods.** Besides the clan-spirits: **Pulura**, Mistress of the Stars and the northern\nlights, honoured at **Dyinglight** with a ring of idols, and paired with **Kostchtchie** the demon\nof cold as the two faces of winter; the **Green Faith**, born in the Forest of Stones, which is\nnow the Stonewilds; **Gorum**, whom Kellids insist was one of their own and who has not denied\nit; **Desna**, whom they see not as an elf but as a Kellid woman on an elk who leads them to\ngame; **Erastil**; **Pharasma** at Raliscrad; and names now half-lost: **Alglenweis**, **Dolok\nDarkfur**, **Kagia**, **Ristrentho**, the **Stag Mother**, **Sturovenen** the Dragoneagle. There\nwas worship of Sarenrae here too, in shrines like the one two days west of Drezen. And there was\nalways Deskari's cult in the Northmounds, driven out by Aroden and never quite gone. The thing\nabout locusts is that you never get all of them.\n\n**Huzuris.** The clans kept the memories of their chiefs and witch-wardens in the skulls of\nanimals bound to the clan's story: the **huzuri**, in which the clan's soul was held. A clan that\nlost its huzuri had lost itself. Some came out with the refugees. Most did not. By their own\ntheology, most of the clans of Sarkoris are dead, and the people walking around under clan names\nare ghosts who have not been told. An old woman of the Fire Circle, asked about this by a\nPathfinder with a notebook, said: \"We were told. We're just not stopping.\"\n\n**The cities.** **Iz**, the capital, a centre of metalwork beside its mines, and the first to\nfall; two-thirds of it went into the rift the day it opened, and the remaining third has been the\ndemons' capital ever since, with embassies. **Undarin**, on the Sarkora, the market where the\nminers of the plateau met the herders of the steppe; the demons found the stockyards useful.\n**Storasta**, where the Sarkora meets the West Sellen, a shaman-ruled city of groves and gardens;\nthe last to fall, its sacred circles held by witches and shamans with druids of Mendev and\nNumeria beside them, and their last stand is the ***Song of Sarkoris***, which is very beautiful.\n**Dyinglight**, in the fens of the far north-west, the spiritual heart of the kingdom; the\npriests of **Pulura's Fall** received a vision and the city emptied before the demons arrived,\nthe single instance in this history of a god warning anyone in time. She is a minor one.\n**Raliscrad**, the trade town where the Sarkora met the Isk, abandoned in such haste that the\nwealth was left on the tables, where the demons found it. **Gundrun**, far to the south-west,\ndestroyed, then rebuilt around 4670 by people who simply came back, and who have survived by\nbeing beneath notice. **Domora** in the west, where a god-caller named Hume called a water spirit\nout of a pool in 4172 and taught the art anew; the pool is still there. **Threshold**, a name at\nthe lip of the canyon country, which the old accounts call a prison-tower where Sarkoris kept its\narcane casters under the eyes of the druids. And under the Wolfscrags, **Jormurdun**, a dwarven\nSky Citadel lost since 1600 AR, whose royal line scattered with the pieces of a broken key.\n\n**The diaspora.** The refugees went everywhere, and everywhere had an opinion. Into Mendev, where\nthe Third Crusade's inquisitors treated their animism as demon-worship and burned them for it.\nInto Numeria. Into Ustalav, where the locals call them **leechfolk** and where the northern\nShudderwood is haunted by Sarkorians the Ustalavs murdered, who have at least stayed to make a\npoint. West to the Mammoth Lords, who share their blood and took them in the way one takes in a\ncousin. And a stubborn handful never left: the folk of Gundrun, the **Tarnshiavs** at Shadow\nSpring in the fen, the Kellids of **Sabertooth Vale** (who are not what they were, and would\nrather you did not ask), and the humans of **Raliscrad** who chose to work for a demon rather than\nrun. Three generations on, \"Sarkorian\" means a great-grandmother's stories, a word or two of\nHallit, a tattoo pattern, a skull in a box, and the certainty that the country over the river is\n*yours* and was taken.\n\nWhat a Fire Circle grandmother keeps, from an inventory a landlord's clerk once tried to make:\n\n> *One (1) skull, elk, wrapped. Not for sale. Not for touching. Not for asking about.*\\\n> *One (1) knife, the good one. One (1) knife, the other one.*\\\n> *Four (4) words of Hallit the grandchildren know. Eleven (11) they do not.*\\\n> *One (1) map of Undarin, drawn from memory, wrong.*\\\n> *One (1) place set at every meal. Not for Aroden. For someone else. Don't ask about that either.*\n\n**Bio hooks from Sarkoris.** A god-caller's granddaughter with the family god's name and no god,\nwhich puts her ahead of most clerics. A huzuri-keeper whose clan is down to four people, three of\nwhom are not speaking. A Fire Circle cook who has never seen the country he calls home and cooks\nit anyway. A Kellid raised by crusaders who burned her parents and taught her to be grateful. A\nSarkorian who came home with the supply wagons because the walls at Drezen are the first walls on\nthis side of the river a Kellid could sleep behind in seventy-five years. A crusader built them.\nA demon kept them. She sleeps fine. Someone from Gundrun, who has lived his whole life inside the\nWound and wants to know what all the fuss is about.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The Worldwound: what Sarkoris became\n\nA hundred and seven years ago a mile-long blight rimmed in black flame opened in the barrows of\nthe Northmounds, and the land came apart around it: a fan of canyons reaching nearly to every\nborder, rivers gone into their beds and staying there, and a fire that has not gone out since.\nThe ground itself shifts in front of you the nearer you go to the rift; at the edges it settles,\nmostly, and the crusade has built its cities on the mostly. Ashstorms come off it. The sky over\nthe centre is poison and coloured lightning. Even the animals are tainted. Every crusader chart of\nit is a copy of a Sarkorian chart, because the crusade has never held anything long enough to\nname it.\n\nThe crusade's recruiters have a sheet for this, *Questions Commonly Asked by Those Considering\nService West of the Sellen*, the most honest document the crusade has ever printed, because it\nwas written by a clerk who had been there:\n\n> *Is it as bad as they say?* Yes.\\\n> *Is the ground safe to walk on?* Until it is not. You will not be warned.\\\n> *What do the demons want?* To watch.\\\n> *Are there people living there?* Yes. Do not assume they want rescuing. Some of them have jobs.\\\n> *What is the pay?* The pay is discussed at the citadel.\\\n> *What should I bring?* Boots. A second pair of boots. Something to be buried with, or a note saying you do not mind.\n\n**The five regions**, as the crusade's scholars divide them, from a safe distance:\n\n- **The Wounded Lands**: the east, and the rift itself, what was the Northmounds, now a canyon a\n  mile wide and dozens of miles long, full of chaos-stuff and bubbling primordial coagulation.\n  **Iz** sits at its heart and **Drezen** on its eastern arm. The crusade's forward citadel and\n  the enemy's capital are on the same limb. The **Marchlands**, badlands, canyon and lava west of\n  Drezen, are what the crusade calls the part of this it has had to learn.\n- **The Stonewilds**: the broken high country to the north, once the **Forest of Stones**,\n  birthplace of the Green Faith. When it fell, a faction of its druids chose undeath rather than\n  defeat, and the **Stonewild Sect** now defends the Stonewilds against demon and living alike,\n  having concluded that the living were the problem. The **Petrified Library** and **Greengrave\n  Keep** are up there. Nobody in Drezen can say who holds either. Nobody in Drezen has\n  volunteered.\n- **Riftshadow**: the river country, the Sarkora and its towns, once the most populous part of\n  the kingdom, now ruins under demonic barges. **Undarin** at its north end, **Storasta** at its\n  south, **Raliscrad** between, and the river running past all three as if nothing had happened.\n- **Frostmire**: the frozen north-west, the **Wolfscrags** with their packs of dire wolves, and\n  **Frostmire Fen**, frigid marshland broken by sulphurous hot springs. Fiendish marsh giants hold\n  Dyinglight and have taken up cannibalism, which the demons find a bit much; hezrou dredge the\n  fen for relics; things called bowel worms live in the water, and the name is descriptive.\n- **The Sarkorian Steppe**: the far west, open, flat, dry, crossed by mammoth and woolly\n  rhinoceros and by the war-bands of the **Mammoth Lords**, who push the border east a mile at a\n  time and lose it again, and have been doing so for a century with the patience of people who\n  have never heard of a crusade. The **Shudderwood**, the one great forest left, fills its\n  southern corner, and the fey there hate everyone now, evenly.\n\n**The rivers.** The **West Sellen** is the eastern boundary and the road the crusade came in by.\nThe **Ahari** ran under Drezen's walls until the Wound opened; its dry bed now cuts the city in\nhalf. The **Sarkora** still flows, out of habit; the **Rolling River** and the **Isk** do not,\nand their dry beds are roads. The **Gray Road** west of Drezen is a dry riverbed named for what\nwalks it. The locals decline to be more specific.\n\n**Who rules.** Nothing, and everything, and it is more stable than Mendev. A loose coalition of\ndemonic warlords, each holding a fief by force or by guile, united only in wanting Golarion to\nsuffer, which as a political programme has the merit of being achievable. The greatest is the\nbalor **Khorramzadeh**, the **Storm King**, who rules Iz and took Kenabres. Next was the marilith\n**Aponavisius**, who took Drezen in 4638 and held it until Rova; she is not in it now, and is\nsomewhere. The most respected is the witch **Areelu Vorlesh**, who held Undarin and is said to\nhave had a hand in opening the Wound. Respected, note, not obeyed. Demons do not obey. They are\nmerely impressed. The lilitu **Minagho** kept Raliscrad, with a staff. Under them, the\n**Templars of the Ivory Labyrinth**, Baphomet's secret society, whose cells hide inside\nrespectable families and who have infiltrated crusader orders before, and the cults of\n**Deskari**, Lord of the Locust Host, whose green-and-red locust wings are the sign of the enemy,\nand whose entire theology is that everything should be eaten. He has been at this since 4606. He\nis not in a hurry. Nobody who is winning is.\n\n**Humans in the Wound.** More than you would think, and fewer than there were. Cultists and\nmercenaries, who will tell you their employers keep their word; chattel kept for labour, food or\nsacrifice, in that order of preference; the willing workers of Raliscrad; the free folk of\nGundrun; the enslaved miners of Iz, who broke every production record under the whip; and the\nthousands who lived in occupied Drezen. A character born inside the Wound is possible. Ask the\nDM. Ask nicely.\n\n**What you meet on the road.** Babau, brimoraks, vrocks, nabasu, shadow demons; derakni in the\nsky; vescavors, colony-swarms that eat metal and skin and are not particular about the order;\nplague steeds; urannags; the fungus called Shax's beard, which grows where it likes; ghouls in the\nchapels; the winter wraiths of the Crown of the World, raised from Drezen's own dead and still,\nin some sense, on the garrison rolls. And seventy-five years of names on the charts, every one of\nthem marking a place someone was sure was safe.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Drezen: the city you are standing in\n\n**What it was.** A citadel-city raised in the hubris of the First Crusade, on the eastern arm of\nthe Wounded Lands, a monument to the proposition that the war was as good as won. In 4638 it was\nbetrayed from within by **Staunton Vhane**, a dwarf paladin turned, and taken by\n**Aponavisius**; a wave of raw chaos turned most of its people into beast-things that ate each\nother, and the corpses were carted to the ice cliffs of the Crown of the World, where they rose\nas winter wraiths. Drezen's dead were put to work before they were cold. **Saint Argil**, the\nPauper Sorcerer, died on the walls covering the flight of those who got out, and was made a saint\nfor it, which is the crusade's pension scheme. The shrines of Iomedae and Sarenrae were made into\nmockeries of themselves, by demons who had clearly studied the originals. For seventy-five years,\nthree generations, it was a demon city of some seven thousand.\n\n**How it was taken.** By a hundred volunteers and the four champions, in one week of Rova: the\ncourtyard on the 8th, the sally port and the Templar's Court on the 9th, three days on the first\nfloor of the citadel, Staunton Vhane dead and buried on the 12th and his glaive unmade in the\nforge, and the dungeons on the 13th, where the banner was. The enemy's catapults were wrecked\nduring the siege, which nobody had thought to do in seventy-five years. **Kamilo Dann**, who held\na dozen wounded in a stone gaol at Vilareth Ford until the ford was retaken, is one of many small\nheroes of that fortnight, and yours can be another. Small is a description of the pay.\n\n**Who runs it now.** In the sense that anyone does.\n\n- **Irabeth Tirabade**, half-orc paladin of the Eagle Watch, Governor. Held Kenabres together\n  with her bare hands and was released by the Queen to ride north. On being handed Drezen: \"I\n  had a city. It fell on me. Give me another.\"\n- **Aron Kir**, former spy, now Warden; keeps the citadel's watch and its secrets, and has the\n  look of a man who has read your file. He has read your file.\n- **Horgus Gwerm**, a Kenabres noble who counted his gold in the rubble and now counts the\n  city's grain. His office door reads: *NO. Come in and I will explain why.*\n- **Anevia Tirabade**, the Governor's wife and the crusade's agent; her spies map the west.\n- **Joran Vhane**, Staunton's kinsman, a dwarven master smith, surrendered in the citadel and\n  now a penitent who dreams of relighting the forge beneath Drezen for good ends. Asked whether\n  that was not what the last Vhane said, he answered, \"He said it better. I'm going to do it.\"\n\nFor new arrivals, the Warden's office issues a single sheet:\n\n> *ON ARRIVAL IN DREZEN*\\\n> *Where do I sleep?* Where you can. The barracks are full. The inns are full. The temple compound has an orphanage, a food bank and an infirmary; qualify for one.\\\n> *Where do I eat?* Rabiah's compound, the kitchen. Do not comment on the angle.\\\n> *Where do I get paid?* The citadel, on completion, on presentation of the work, on the Warden's word that the work was done. The Warden's word is not for sale. Do not ask what it is for.\\\n> *Is the South Bank safe?* The South Bank is across the river.\\\n> *Is the river safe?* The river is dry.\\\n> *Is the dry river safe?* Use the bridges.\\\n> *Who do I report a demon to?* Anyone. Loudly.\\\n> *Who do I report a crusader to?* The Warden. Quietly.\\\n> *Where is the Queen's steward?* Nerosyan. He was here for an afternoon. We have his chair.\n\n**The lie of the city.** The citadel on the high ground, the Preservers' compound north-east of\nit, the temple compound and Rabiah's crooked ring quarter on the eastern ridge, the Celeste\nMarket packed between the citadel wall and the dry riverbed, and across the riverbed the **South\nBank**, scrap yards and rope-walks that spent three generations under demon rule and have not\nbeen searched. **Cinder Row** runs through it. That is where the work is. The rest is on the [Map tab](#/map/drezen),\nand the map is in this document's voice, so you have been warned.\n\n**The roads.** East and south, the road down to **Kenabres**: **Keeper's Canyon**, where two\narmies camped, one of which is still there in pieces; **Vilareth Ford**; about a week's march\nfor a column, less for a rider. West, the road to the **Hidden Temple**: two days on foot, with\nnothing on it. \"Nothing\" is a technical term. Beyond that, the **Gray Road**, the **Weeping\nHills**, the lava country, which the company will not be asked to visit, until it is. North,\nthe **Lake Lost to the Sun**, named for the light that does not reach it. A hex on the\nMarchlands chart is twelve miles, or a hard day's march.\n\n**What Drezen feels like.** A garrison learning to be a town, and the town learning what a\ngarrison thinks a town is for. Acid-scarred stone. Two temples facing each other across a\nstreet, both newer than the season. More buildings than people, and a standing policy of not\nfinding out what is in them. Every party that goes west leaves by the western gate and is\ncounted home through the same arch. The ledger at the gate has three columns: *Out*, *In*, and\n*Shrinkage*. It is the most complete record Drezen keeps.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Building the bio\n\nYou do not need a life story. You need enough that the DM can hurt you with it. Think about all\nfive of these, then fold two or three into a bio of a few paragraphs and leave the rest for the\ntable to find out.\n\n- **Where are you from?** A quarter, a village, a cave, a boat. Who is still there, and do they\n  know where you are?\n- **What are you good at, and why?** Not the class. The thing the class grew out of, and who\n  paid for the lesson.\n- **What are you afraid of, and why?** Everyone in Mendev shares the same three fears. The\n  fourth is the useful one, and it can be small. Bells. Being counted.\n- **Why are you in Drezen right now?** Not why you came. Why you are still here, this week, when\n  the road south is open and nobody checks papers.\n- **Where do you see yourself in five levels?** Nobody in Drezen asks, because the answer is the\n  cemetery. Ask anyway. A stall in the market. A squad. A skull carried home to a river that runs\n  again.\n\nIf it helps: one thing you carry that is not on the equipment list, and the gesture you make\nwhen you pass the temples.\n\n### Naming by people\n\n- **Native Mendevian**: Iobarian roots, sturdy and slightly Slavic-sounding, the names of people\n  who expected to be here a while. *Hulrun Shappok, Irabeth Tirabade, Anevia, Sosiel Vaenic,\n  Nestrin Alodae, Kamilo Dann, Trudean Delashaw, Jeskar Hinton, Tilda Merrow.*\n- **Crusader stock**: southern names from anywhere: Taldan (*Galfrey, Aron Kir, Aldrich Thorne,\n  Brannic Vayne, Dyre, Marcovina*), Chelaxian, Andoren, Varisian. A family name that is a place\n  (\"of Lauterbury\") is a knightly affectation, and usually a place that has never heard of them.\n- **Sarkorian / Kellid**: short and hard: *Dron, Gurog, Kronug, Belka, Inkit, Yala*; the clan\n  name over the given name; longer and stranger for the notable: *Uloric Dziergas, Domora Hume,\n  Khraigorr Half-Face, Tarnshiav, Widowknife, Farheaven.* Nobody in Sarkoris was called\n  something they could not shout across a valley.\n- **Mongrelmen of Neathholm**: single, plain, given underground by people who did not expect\n  anyone above to ask: *Lann, Chusul, Opoli, Chyrrik.*\n- **Drezen-born**: whatever the demons let them be called. *Fenna Tusk* is the one example, and\n  she seems to have chosen it herself.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Sources, and what was left out\n\n**Paizo.** *The Inner Sea World Guide*; *Pathfinder Campaign Setting: The Worldwound*; *Lost\nKingdoms* and *Lost Cities of Golarion*; *Wrath of the Righteous*, *The Worldwound Incursion* and\n*Sword of Valor*; *Siege of the Diamond City*; *Inner Sea Gods*; *Lost Omens Gods & Magic*;\n*Dragons Revisited*; *Blood of Fiends*; the Pathfinder Society material on Jormurdun and the\nStarrise Spire. All checked against PathfinderWiki, September 2026.\n\n**The Fifth Crusade.** Its dramatis personae, lore, calendar and maps, the Book I to III source,\nand the secrets *Following the Flame*, *The Ballad of the Wardstone's Champions* and *The\nRiftwardens' Children*.\n\n**Invented here, and yours to strike.** The quoted lines from named people, the Preservers'\nrecruiting bill, the pilgrims' pamphlet, the recruiters' sheet, the Warden's arrival sheet, the\nthree kitchen sayings, the chair for Aroden at the Argent Shield, the grandmother's inventory,\nand the gate ledger's three columns. None of them contradict the chronicle. None of them are in\nit either. They become this table's canon when a chapter uses them, and not before.\n\n**Deliberately excluded**, because it has not happened yet or is not yet known at the table:\neverything Paizo has published about the years after 4713, the deep history of Threshold and\nAreelu Vorlesh, and *Wrath of the Righteous* Books IV to VI. If a player has read ahead, the bio\nshould not show it.","text":"Where You Come From: A Character Bio Helper The Real Work. The lands around Drezen, for anyone writing a character who is standing in the city in the autumn of 4713 AR and has not yet worked out why. This is a handout for building a backstory. It is not a chapter, not a rulebook, and not a promise that any of it will still be true by Kuthona. It gathers what passes for fact about the country around Drezen (the Worldwound, old Sarkoris, Mendev and its capital Nerosyan) from Paizo's published setting and from the crusade's own chronicle at The Fifth Crusade, and it stops at the present day. Which source wins. Where Paizo and the chronicle differ, the chronicle governs at this table. Where the chronicle is silent, Paizo's setting stands. Where both are silent, make something up and tell the DM, so that it can be written down and later contradicted by a dice roll. How to use it. Skim §1 so you know what everyone in the city knows. Read the one or two sections that match where your character is from. Then see §8. The map of Drezen is on this site's Map tab, in the same voice as this document. The general situation, for those in a hurry. A god was due back in 4606 to settle everything. He died instead. A hole opened where he was supposed to stand, and everything since has been people building walls in front of the hole and demons taking the walls down, for a hundred and seven years, with great sincerity on both sides. The demons are enjoying it more. You have been hired to do the part of this that does not fit in a song. --- 1. The moment you are standing in It is Neth, 4713 AR . Late autumn, with the first hard frosts on the Marchlands and the southern horizon lit orange after dark. Everyone in Drezen carries these facts, mostly the way one carries a bad tooth. Kenabres fell on Armasse, the 16th of Arodus. Iomedae's own feast day. The Storm King came over the walls, the silver dragon Terendelev died in the sky above the city, the great bridge went down, and the wardstone in the Kite was shattered. The crusade's northern wall for a century fell in an afternoon. Prelate Hulrun had spent fifty years burning people to keep the demons out. The demons killed him first. The wardstones are gone. A week later, at the Gray Garrison , four strangers out of the rubble broke the corrupted fragment before the enemy could turn it against the army gathering at the border, and every wardstone along the West Sellen went dark with it. The border that held for seventy-five years is now a river. The power the stones held went into the four. The church has ruled this a miracle. The Riftwardens have asked to see the paperwork. The four are Rabiah , a sorcerer of sixteen; Harlock Greyforge , a half-orc paladin who carries a sword that talks; Lupenor Celest , an elven archer; and Varic Sarian , a priest of Sarenrae. Mendev calls them the Wardstone's Champions, and there is a ballad. You will hear it in every tavern between here and Egede, sung by people who could not find Drezen on a chart, and the verse about the forty at the door gets longer every month. The four are away in the west now, days out of contact. They do not otherwise come into this document. You will not be working for them. You will be working for the people they left behind, who are less famous and pay in coin. Queen Galfrey rode north to Kenabres herself, and sent the champions to Drezen with a hundred volunteers. They marched out on the 30th of Arodus, took Vilareth Ford back on the road, declined to walk into Keeper's Canyon in the dark, and came within sight of Drezen's walls on the 6th of Rova. Most of the hundred are alive. Nobody had planned for that, and the barracks are short of beds. Drezen changed hands on Fire Day, the 13th of Rova, 4713. The citadel that fell to the demons in 4638 is a crusader city again. The Sword of Valor , the banner lost when it fell, was carried out of the dungeons beneath it, flawless after seventy-five years in demon hands. Staunton Vhane , the dwarf who betrayed the city three generations ago, is buried here. The banner came through the occupation without a stain. Nothing else did. Sir Aldrich Thorne came from the Queen the next day, by teleportation, with two guards. He saw the city passed to Irabeth Tirabade to govern, declined a meal, and left the same afternoon. He has not been back. Ask anyone in Drezen what the Queen's man thought of the place and they will tell you how long he stayed. The crusade holds the walls and the citadel. It does not yet hold every quarter. The people holding the rest are hiring. You are what they hired. If your character has been in Drezen more than a week, they have also heard: that the demons themselves have posted a thousand platinum for a succubus somewhere in the west, more than the crusade has ever offered for anything; that a shrine of Sarenrae two days down the western road has been reopened and is kept by a priest named Rothin Vald ; that the South Bank has not been searched; and that Horgus Gwerm , the quartermaster, is browbeating the crown for grain. His last dispatch is public knowledge because he read it aloud in the market first: Your Majesty. Drezen holds. Drezen has four hundred mouths more than it had on the 13th and the same grain. I am told the crown's stores are committed to Kenabres. I am told a great many things. Send wheat, or send fewer people, or send Sir Aldrich back to explain it to them; he will not need long. Gwerm, Quartermaster. The dates that matter | Year (AR) | Event | Declared | What it did | |---|---|---|---| | 3672 | Sarkoris founded when the Kellid clans broke the frost giants of the Northmounds. | A kingdom. | Built a kingdom on top of the barrows. | | 4433 | Aroden drives the cult of Deskari out of the Northmounds into the Lake of Mists and Veils. | The end of the cult. | Moved the cult. | | 4601 | The last prince of Mendev dies on an embassy to Sarkoris. His daughter Galfrey is crowned. | Mysterious. | Still mysterious. | | 4606 | Aroden, prophesied to return, dies. The Worldwound opens. Iz falls first. | The end of the world. | Just the north. | | 4622–4630 | First Crusade. Demons pushed back to the Northmounds. Drezen founded. Nerosyan walled. | A success. | Told the demons how the crusade fights. | | 4638 | Drezen falls to the marilith Aponavisius , betrayed from within by Staunton Vhane, a paladin. | A tragedy. | Also that. | | 4638–4645 | Second Crusade. Sarkoris written off. The wardstones raised; the first in the Kite at Kenabres, 4639. | A failure. | Held the line for seventy-five years. | | 4665–4668 | Third Crusade. Demonic infiltration answered with witch hunts. Hundreds burned at Kenabres. | A purification. | Burned Mendevians. Ran out of Mendevians. Stopped. | | c. 4670 | Gundrun, in the far south-west, rebuilt by Sarkorian survivors. | Nothing; nobody noticed. | The only free town in the Wound. | | 4684 | The Demonscope placed at Clydwell Keep. | Vigilance. | A very good view of the demons. | | 4692–4707 | Fourth Crusade. The balor Khorramzadeh , the Storm King, cracks the Kenabres wardstone. Fifteen years. The Order of Heralds ends the purges. | A stalemate. | Everyone went home tired. The Heralds got this cell in a table. | | 4713, 16 Arodus | Kenabres falls. | The end of the world. | See 4606. | | 4713, 23 Arodus | The last wardstone fragment destroyed by its own defenders. | Heresy, for about a day. | It worked. | | 4713, 13 Rova | Drezen retaken. | A turning point. | Four hundred extra mouths and the same grain. | | 4713, Neth | Now. | | | --- 2. Mendev: the crusader state The land. The northern end of the continent: snow-covered mountains, icy steppe, and the great evergreen Estrovian Forest across the centre and south. North, the ice cliffs of the Crown of the World. East, the Lake of Mists and Veils , which every crusader crossed to get here. South, across the rivers, Numeria , a harsh country of savages and alien metal that Mendevians want nothing to do with. West, the West Sellen River , and on its far bank the Worldwound. There is almost no arable land. For a century the crusade has been fed by boats, crusaders and grain coming up the Sellen and across the lake, arriving weekly at Egede . The grain is inspected. The crusaders are not. Three kinds of Mendevian. Nobody in Mendev is just \"a Mendevian.\" Ask which. - The natives. Descended from Iobarian exiles, semi-nomadic once, and cousins to the Sarkorians across the river. Many still keep the old animist faith or the Green Faith , quietly. The crusaders have treated them as second-class for a hundred years and in the Third Crusade burned them for heresy. A native Mendevian character has a grandmother who remembers who informed on whom. The crown kept no such list. The grandmothers did. - The crusaders and their children. From Cheliax, Isger, Andoran, Taldor and everywhere else. The first wave were the finest people of the south, clerics and paladins and knights who sold their lands to come. By the Fourth Crusade the boats were bringing mercenaries, dispossessed nobles, political undesirables and men let out of prison on condition they went north, and these now outnumber the pious. A crusader's-child character can be third-generation Mendevian and still be a foreigner to the natives, whose great-uncle their grandfather may have denounced. - The Sarkorians. Kellid refugees of the lost kingdom, three generations in exile. See §5. In Nerosyan they have a quarter of their own along the western wall, the Fire Circle . Others took the caves of Saresp Mountain in the far east, or scattered into Numeria, Ustalav and beyond. And among them: tieflings in unusual numbers (Mendev is home to many asura-spawn, and the Wound breeds pitborn); dwarves whose ancestors fled the lost Sky Citadel of Jormurdun with a royal claim and a piece of a broken key, and who mostly live poor in the Fleshward slum of Chesed, still holding the piece; halflings; half-elves; and beneath Kenabres, the mongrelmen , the warped descendants of First Crusade soldiers whom the Wound changed and whom their own comrades then tried to kill. Rule. A monarchy under Queen Galfrey , the Sword of Iomedae, a Taldan woman who looks to be in her middle forties and is over a hundred years old, held there by the sun orchid elixir. Crowned in 4601 as a paladin of Aroden; five years later Aroden died, and she retrained herself as Iomedae's paladin with the brisk practicality of a woman whose first god had let her down. She ordered the retreat from Sarkoris in 4638 so the wardstones could be raised, and has been hated for it since by the descendants of the people it kept alive. Her word is law within Nerosyan's walls. Beyond them, every commander of every city, fort and army interprets her commands a little differently. Until this summer the second most powerful person in the kingdom was Prelate Hulrun Shappok of Kenabres, whose witch-burnings as a young man started the Third Crusade, and who only in his last years began to understand the shame he had brought on the faith. He was working on it. Faith. Iomedae , the Inheritor, is the crusade. Her white-and-gold banner is the one all crusaders nominally fight under, her cathedral is the centre of Nerosyan, and her holy day Armasse is the day Kenabres fell. She inherited her portfolio from Aroden, who died on the day he was due to return. The Chapel of the Argent Shield on the Egede road, once his, still keeps a chair for him by the altar. Nobody sits in it. Nobody has moved it either. Beside her: Sarenrae , whose priests march with the columns and heal what the columns leave; Shelyn , Desna , Erastil , Abadar , Pharasma ; Gorum in the barracks of Egede; Cayden Cailean , whose neighbourhood in Nerosyan has the city's best-attended services. The Green Faith and the animist ways of the natives survive, and are looked at sideways. Every Mendevian, pious or not, knows the gesture: fingers to the lips, then pressed to the sword-pommel. Ask a Mendevian what it does. Watch them do it again while they think. Three sayings from any Mendevian kitchen: Aroden is coming. Set a place. The wall is only as good as the priest is sober. Pray to Iomedae. Pay Abadar. Drink with Cayden. Bury with Pharasma. Ask the trees when nobody's looking. Orders and outfits. A crusader belongs to something, or pretends to. - The Eagle Watch : the crusaders of Kenabres. Irabeth Tirabade commanded them at the Defender's Heart; Anevia , her wife, served among them. - The Order of Heralds : curbed the Third Crusade's purges and turned the war back outward. Still the conscience of the crusade. See the table above for their reward. - The Riftwardens : a secretive society of planar scholars who want the Worldwound closed , not merely held, based in Kenabres's Librarium of the Broken Black Wing , which has since partly collapsed. Aravashnial , an elven wizard blinded in the fall of Kenabres and since healed by the Queen's own priests, is one. Their mark is the Seeker's Spiral. Do not give it to a new character without asking. - Iomedae's Preservers : the newest of the orders, founded this autumn by Harlock Greyforge and headquartered in Drezen. Five squads sworn to hold the city. Their recruiting bill is nailed to the citadel gate: IOMEDAE'S PRESERVERS seek the steadfast. Three days' trial. Bring your own boots. Food provided on the third day. Those who leave before the third day are asked to return the boots if they were not their own. Apply to Dame Elara Dawnstrider, who will know if you are lying. - The Kenabres Wolves : the spies and scouts, Aron Kir 's people. He is Warden of Drezen now. - The Order of Holy Sentinels : guardians of the Demonscope at Clydwell Keep , the greatest of the border forts. Every fort on the old wardstone line is named for a dead general. - The knightly orders : the Emerald Sword, the Flaming Lance, the Rampant Dragon, the Sunrise Sword, the Guild of Diggers and Sappers, and dozens more. Some are ancient. Some were invented on the boat, because the heralds of Iomedae cannot check every newcomer's claim to a title. The heralds know this. The knights know the heralds know. A title is a lie everyone has agreed on, and Mendev has agreed on a great many. - The Low Templars : the crusade coined the name for its mercenaries and sellswords, then noticed it had coined a name for most of the crusade, and kept it. - The Pathfinder Society : in Nerosyan since the Queen gave them the Starrise Spire in 4707, sending expeditions into the Wound disguised as mercenary companies. - The inquisitors : Hulrun's people. Fewer than they were, and quieter since Kenabres. Banners. Any crusader stronghold is a riot of colour, flags from hundreds of clans, counties, kingdoms and families competing for room. The banners that matter are earned on the field and take the form of Iomedae's own symbols, the sword , the shield or the sunburst , granted to soldiers of any rank. It is the one honour in Mendev that cannot be bought, and it is a piece of cloth. Speech. Mendevians speak Taldane (Common); the natives and the Sarkorians also speak Hallit , the Kellid tongue, which has no written form. A gaffle or a rip is a scam. There is no Mendevian word for an honest bargain. They use the Taldane, carefully. The wardstones, and their absence. For seventy-five years the border was a line of rune-carved menhirs along the West Sellen and the Moutray, fed with nexovar from the hills of the Egelsee, maintained by daily prayer and a yearly ritual, and housed in a chain of forts. Nothing chaotic or evil could pass between them, and no teleportation could cross the line. They are gone since Arodus. The forts are still garrisoned. The prayers are still said every morning over stones that no longer hear them. A character who served on the line has lost the whole meaning of their posting and kept the posting. The pay still comes. --- 3. Nerosyan: the Diamond of the North The capital, and the crusade's fortress. Before the Worldwound it was a run-down river town of pirates; the First Crusade widened its streets, walled it, and gave it a holy purpose, and the pirates' grandchildren are now the city guard. About sixty-five thousand people, nearly all human. Where it sits. At the junction where the Egelsee River runs into the northernmost branch of the West Sellen . The Egelsee is the city's only clean water; the Sellen comes down too close to the Wound to drink. Everything outside the walls has been burned flat, by demonic raids and by the defenders, so that nothing approaches unseen. Nobody now remembers which fires were whose. How it is built. A diamond between the two rivers, so that every wall has water in front of it. The walls are twenty feet thick, and the wall-tops are roofed , sloped and cut with arrow slits, because the enemy flies. At the centre stands the Cruciform Cathedral of Iomedae, and the avenues run wide and straight from it so that a sortie can reach any wall in minutes, and so that the cathedral can see every door. Four towers hold the points: the Battle Tower in the north, the most heavily fortified structure in Mendev; the Woundward Tower , holding the city's wardstone and its best archers; the Confluence Tower , guarding the rivers and Crusader's Quay; and the Egelsee Tower , the cavalry's ground. Above it all burns the Starrise Spire , older than the walls, with a ball of light floating over it that has not gone out in centuries: a beacon to the crusade and a warning to the Wound, which has had a century to get used to it. The districts. Battle, Egelsee, Confluence and Woundward are the four quarters. Within them: Pilgrim's Rest , where the endless stream of Iomedaean pilgrims lodges; Bannerbright , where the orders keep their houses and their feuds; the Two Cs , Cayden Cailean's neighbourhood, which is where the taverns are; the Fire Circle along the western wall, where the Sarkorian refugees keep their shops and eating houses in the old style, facing the wall, facing home. Hank the Alerunner's hideout is a tavern full of relics of the pirate days. Ancuin's Brewery has been the crusaders' favourite for a hundred years and is the institution with the best record in Mendev. From the pamphlet handed to pilgrims at the Sellen Docks, fourth printing: WELCOME TO NEROSYAN, DIAMOND OF THE NORTH. Pilgrims will find lodging in Pilgrim's Rest, at rates set by the crown. Pilgrims will find other lodging in the Two Cs, at rates set by the Two Cs. The Cathedral is open at all hours. The Woundward Tower is not a viewing platform. Please do not feed the Sarkorians; they have their own quarter and their own food and they would like you to know that. Recruiters are stationed at every gate and are not permitted to follow you into the Cathedral. They know this. Walk quickly. Who runs it. The Queen, from her palace, counselled by an angel named Opaline , who has presumably seen the whole plan and is not sharing. Around her, Lord Inquisitor Casori , Captain Jaspar of the Crusader Heralds, and Venture-Captain Jorsal of Lauterbury of the Pathfinders. What has happened lately. For years the city has been harried by things that come up the rivers, merrow and scrags and grindylows in the service of the hezrou that rules ruined Storasta downriver, and there are stories of a larger siege this year, turned back with the Pathfinders in the thick of it, which the Pathfinders tell. The wagons of conscripts and supply have been rolling north to Kenabres and on to Drezen since Rova. They leave Nerosyan with twelve conscripts and reach Drezen with nine. The quartermaster's ledger records this as shrinkage . Bio hooks from Nerosyan. A dock-hand who has unloaded a hundred boatloads of heroes and watched none come back down. A cathedral acolyte who has never been outside the walls and believes everything, so far. A Two Cs brawler with a holy excuse. A child of the Fire Circle who speaks Hallit at home and Taldane in the street and lies in both. A brewery drayman, the most respected trade in the city. A conscript caught stealing bread and put on a supply wagon, who slipped away the moment it passed Drezen's gate. That is how Tam , a water-carrier in Drezen, arrived. Asked about it, Tam said, \"I was shrinkage.\" --- 4. Kenabres and the border: what fell, and what stands Kenabres was the crusade's northern city: twelve thousand people on tiers rising from the eastern plain, its western edge a cliff above the West Sellen. Old Kenabres at the top held the oldest families and the administration, which were the same families; the Ring District was the rest of the original town; New Kenabres was built for refugees of the lost settlements, and the Gate District ten years later when New Kenabres overflowed, because the settlements kept getting lost. The Truestone Quarry , ten miles east, sent stone weekly to the walls. The road to Egede is 225 miles and ten days by caravan, past the Chapel of the Argent Shield , with its chair. It was also the crusade's conscience and its shame, which in Kenabres were the same building: the seat of Hulrun's inquisition, where the generals Dyre and Marcovina fought the Prelate for every soldier he wanted kept home to hunt heretics instead of demons. Heretics were easier to find. The Kite held the first wardstone ever raised. The Blackwing Library held the crusade's greatest store of learning on the demons and the planes. All of it was consulted. What is left. Half the population, about six and a half thousand, in a city split by rifts, its cathedral and the Kite destroyed, the library partly collapsed, and the Queen's own appointee in the Prelate's chair. The Defender's Heart , the fortified inn where Irabeth held the survivors together, is the heart of what remains. When the cathedral fell, the people went to the pub, and the pub held. The chalkboard by its door has not been wiped since Arodus: BEDS: none. SOUP: yes. WATER: boil it. MISSING: ask Anevia. FOUND: ask Anevia. MONGRELMEN ARE CUSTOMERS. Signed, I. Tirabade. (beneath, in another hand) she means it The assault tore open the tunnels under the city, and out of them came the mongrelmen of Neathholm , a hamlet of a few dozen on an island in an underground lake, under its one-eyed chief Chusul . They lived under the holiest city in the north for ninety years and the holiest city never once looked down. The road north. Valas's Gift , a village of a couple of hundred north of Kenabres, named for a holy man and its red-water springs. The springs were red before the war. The Vineyard Hills Waystation , a day's ride south-east, a crossroads outpost well behind the old wardstone line, and attacked anyway. The Estrovian Forest , where few go willingly: hernes, stag-horned things that were once trespassing huntsmen, cursed by druids who then died of their own curse; witchwargs; a pack of owlbears that may be ghosts; taiga giants; and the werebear-kin, the only guides who will take you through, and the only inhabitants who could eat you and generally choose not to. Clydwell Keep and the named forts, at full strength, guarding the memory of a line. And far off, the cursed ruins of Icerift Castle , about which people do not talk. Egede , on the lake. The second city, nearly forty thousand, the end of the River Road and the first Mendevian ground most crusaders set foot on. Where exhausted soldiers are sent to rest, which means prayer at the temples or the covert brothels. The temples know about the brothels and the brothels know about the temples and both keep the other's hours. Where merchants have made an industry of bankrupting pilgrims on their way to the war, so that the demons get them pre-stripped. Aroden drowned a demon lord here once, and Egedens celebrate Drowning Day for twenty-four hours in a way outsiders find disturbing. They do not set a place for him. Egede has always been practical. Bio hooks from Kenabres. Where were you on Armasse? In the Gate District when the bridge came down. In the quarry caravan, ten miles out, watching the sky do something the sky should not. In the cells of the inquisition, which turned out to be the safest place in the city. In the tunnels, and you did not know the tunnels were there. Among the refugees in the Defender's Heart when everyone decided, on no evidence, that things were about to get better. A city watchman with twelve years' service and a burn scar you explain differently every time. A carpenter who lost workshop, tools and wife in an afternoon and has been very calm about it. A mongrelman of Neathholm above ground for the first time, finding it overrated. --- 5. Old Sarkoris: the country that was For a thousand years before the Worldwound, the land west of the Sellen was Sarkoris : a savage, proud, painted Kellid kingdom of fiercely independent clans, founded in 3672 when the tribes broke the frost giants of the Northmounds and took the Northmounds as a prize. Everything on the crusade's charts west of the river is Sarkorian, because the crusade has never had anything better to put in its place. You can conquer a country three times and still be using its maps. The people. Kellids : lean, dark-haired, light-brown-skinned, with dark eyes and scars they are proud of. A Kellid without scars is thought to have lived too comfortably, and a Kellid who has lived comfortably is asked at whose expense. They dress in leather, fur and hide, and wear zoic fetishes , charms of twine, hair and stone that their druids enchant. They value strength, bravery and cunning; they have no patience for compromise or weak leaders; they carry only what the clan needs; they respect Ulfen and dwarves and distrust magic and machines, having lived between Irrisen's witches and Numeria's engines and seen what each does to a neighbour. The clan comes before the self. Names are short and percussive: Dron , Gurog , Kronug ; Belka , Inkit , Yala . Second names are for the distinguished; a clan name matters more than either. Names of record run longer and stranger: Uloric Dziergas the warlord, Domora Hume the god-caller, Khraigorr Half-Face , who got the name honestly, the Tarnshiavs of Dyinglight, the Widowknife clanhold, the Farheaven clan. The clans and their rulers. Sarkoris was never one thing, which is why it took the demons almost forty years to finish it. Each clan-hold had its clanliege ; the great cities had their witch-wardens ; warlords rose and fell. The mystics of Sarkoris warned, in the years before 4606, of ascendant chaos and a thinning between the worlds. They were right about everything. No one listened, because Aroden was coming, and a place had been set. God-calling. The heart of Sarkorian religion. A god-caller binds a spirit, the clan's own patron, usually a native beast made strange, a bear with feathers, a wolf with wings, and calls it into the world, where the clan worships it as their god. The god turns up. This is the whole of the doctrine and the whole of the appeal. Southern churches call it the worship of false gods, on the authority of true ones who have not been seen since 4606. Many of the clan-gods were destroyed or driven off in the first years of the Wound. The demons could find them. A Sarkorian of god-calling blood may carry a name for a god no one has seen in a hundred years, and a grudge on its behalf. The old gods. Besides the clan-spirits: Pulura , Mistress of the Stars and the northern lights, honoured at Dyinglight with a ring of idols, and paired with Kostchtchie the demon of cold as the two faces of winter; the Green Faith , born in the Forest of Stones, which is now the Stonewilds; Gorum , whom Kellids insist was one of their own and who has not denied it; Desna , whom they see not as an elf but as a Kellid woman on an elk who leads them to game; Erastil ; Pharasma at Raliscrad; and names now half-lost: Alglenweis , Dolok Darkfur , Kagia , Ristrentho , the Stag Mother , Sturovenen the Dragoneagle. There was worship of Sarenrae here too, in shrines like the one two days west of Drezen. And there was always Deskari's cult in the Northmounds, driven out by Aroden and never quite gone. The thing about locusts is that you never get all of them. Huzuris. The clans kept the memories of their chiefs and witch-wardens in the skulls of animals bound to the clan's story: the huzuri , in which the clan's soul was held. A clan that lost its huzuri had lost itself. Some came out with the refugees. Most did not. By their own theology, most of the clans of Sarkoris are dead, and the people walking around under clan names are ghosts who have not been told. An old woman of the Fire Circle, asked about this by a Pathfinder with a notebook, said: \"We were told. We're just not stopping.\" The cities. Iz , the capital, a centre of metalwork beside its mines, and the first to fall; two-thirds of it went into the rift the day it opened, and the remaining third has been the demons' capital ever since, with embassies. Undarin , on the Sarkora, the market where the miners of the plateau met the herders of the steppe; the demons found the stockyards useful. Storasta , where the Sarkora meets the West Sellen, a shaman-ruled city of groves and gardens; the last to fall, its sacred circles held by witches and shamans with druids of Mendev and Numeria beside them, and their last stand is the Song of Sarkoris , which is very beautiful. Dyinglight , in the fens of the far north-west, the spiritual heart of the kingdom; the priests of Pulura's Fall received a vision and the city emptied before the demons arrived, the single instance in this history of a god warning anyone in time. She is a minor one. Raliscrad , the trade town where the Sarkora met the Isk, abandoned in such haste that the wealth was left on the tables, where the demons found it. Gundrun , far to the south-west, destroyed, then rebuilt around 4670 by people who simply came back, and who have survived by being beneath notice. Domora in the west, where a god-caller named Hume called a water spirit out of a pool in 4172 and taught the art anew; the pool is still there. Threshold , a name at the lip of the canyon country, which the old accounts call a prison-tower where Sarkoris kept its arcane casters under the eyes of the druids. And under the Wolfscrags, Jormurdun , a dwarven Sky Citadel lost since 1600 AR, whose royal line scattered with the pieces of a broken key. The diaspora. The refugees went everywhere, and everywhere had an opinion. Into Mendev, where the Third Crusade's inquisitors treated their animism as demon-worship and burned them for it. Into Numeria. Into Ustalav, where the locals call them leechfolk and where the northern Shudderwood is haunted by Sarkorians the Ustalavs murdered, who have at least stayed to make a point. West to the Mammoth Lords, who share their blood and took them in the way one takes in a cousin. And a stubborn handful never left: the folk of Gundrun, the Tarnshiavs at Shadow Spring in the fen, the Kellids of Sabertooth Vale (who are not what they were, and would rather you did not ask), and the humans of Raliscrad who chose to work for a demon rather than run. Three generations on, \"Sarkorian\" means a great-grandmother's stories, a word or two of Hallit, a tattoo pattern, a skull in a box, and the certainty that the country over the river is yours and was taken. What a Fire Circle grandmother keeps, from an inventory a landlord's clerk once tried to make: One (1) skull, elk, wrapped. Not for sale. Not for touching. Not for asking about. One (1) knife, the good one. One (1) knife, the other one. Four (4) words of Hallit the grandchildren know. Eleven (11) they do not. One (1) map of Undarin, drawn from memory, wrong. One (1) place set at every meal. Not for Aroden. For someone else. Don't ask about that either. Bio hooks from Sarkoris. A god-caller's granddaughter with the family god's name and no god, which puts her ahead of most clerics. A huzuri-keeper whose clan is down to four people, three of whom are not speaking. A Fire Circle cook who has never seen the country he calls home and cooks it anyway. A Kellid raised by crusaders who burned her parents and taught her to be grateful. A Sarkorian who came home with the supply wagons because the walls at Drezen are the first walls on this side of the river a Kellid could sleep behind in seventy-five years. A crusader built them. A demon kept them. She sleeps fine. Someone from Gundrun, who has lived his whole life inside the Wound and wants to know what all the fuss is about. --- 6. The Worldwound: what Sarkoris became A hundred and seven years ago a mile-long blight rimmed in black flame opened in the barrows of the Northmounds, and the land came apart around it: a fan of canyons reaching nearly to every border, rivers gone into their beds and staying there, and a fire that has not gone out since. The ground itself shifts in front of you the nearer you go to the rift; at the edges it settles, mostly, and the crusade has built its cities on the mostly. Ashstorms come off it. The sky over the centre is poison and coloured lightning. Even the animals are tainted. Every crusader chart of it is a copy of a Sarkorian chart, because the crusade has never held anything long enough to name it. The crusade's recruiters have a sheet for this, Questions Commonly Asked by Those Considering Service West of the Sellen , the most honest document the crusade has ever printed, because it was written by a clerk who had been there: Is it as bad as they say? Yes. Is the ground safe to walk on? Until it is not. You will not be warned. What do the demons want? To watch. Are there people living there? Yes. Do not assume they want rescuing. Some of them have jobs. What is the pay? The pay is discussed at the citadel. What should I bring? Boots. A second pair of boots. Something to be buried with, or a note saying you do not mind. The five regions , as the crusade's scholars divide them, from a safe distance: - The Wounded Lands : the east, and the rift itself, what was the Northmounds, now a canyon a mile wide and dozens of miles long, full of chaos-stuff and bubbling primordial coagulation. Iz sits at its heart and Drezen on its eastern arm. The crusade's forward citadel and the enemy's capital are on the same limb. The Marchlands , badlands, canyon and lava west of Drezen, are what the crusade calls the part of this it has had to learn. - The Stonewilds : the broken high country to the north, once the Forest of Stones , birthplace of the Green Faith. When it fell, a faction of its druids chose undeath rather than defeat, and the Stonewild Sect now defends the Stonewilds against demon and living alike, having concluded that the living were the problem. The Petrified Library and Greengrave Keep are up there. Nobody in Drezen can say who holds either. Nobody in Drezen has volunteered. - Riftshadow : the river country, the Sarkora and its towns, once the most populous part of the kingdom, now ruins under demonic barges. Undarin at its north end, Storasta at its south, Raliscrad between, and the river running past all three as if nothing had happened. - Frostmire : the frozen north-west, the Wolfscrags with their packs of dire wolves, and Frostmire Fen , frigid marshland broken by sulphurous hot springs. Fiendish marsh giants hold Dyinglight and have taken up cannibalism, which the demons find a bit much; hezrou dredge the fen for relics; things called bowel worms live in the water, and the name is descriptive. - The Sarkorian Steppe : the far west, open, flat, dry, crossed by mammoth and woolly rhinoceros and by the war-bands of the Mammoth Lords , who push the border east a mile at a time and lose it again, and have been doing so for a century with the patience of people who have never heard of a crusade. The Shudderwood , the one great forest left, fills its southern corner, and the fey there hate everyone now, evenly. The rivers. The West Sellen is the eastern boundary and the road the crusade came in by. The Ahari ran under Drezen's walls until the Wound opened; its dry bed now cuts the city in half. The Sarkora still flows, out of habit; the Rolling River and the Isk do not, and their dry beds are roads. The Gray Road west of Drezen is a dry riverbed named for what walks it. The locals decline to be more specific. Who rules. Nothing, and everything, and it is more stable than Mendev. A loose coalition of demonic warlords, each holding a fief by force or by guile, united only in wanting Golarion to suffer, which as a political programme has the merit of being achievable. The greatest is the balor Khorramzadeh , the Storm King , who rules Iz and took Kenabres. Next was the marilith Aponavisius , who took Drezen in 4638 and held it until Rova; she is not in it now, and is somewhere. The most respected is the witch Areelu Vorlesh , who held Undarin and is said to have had a hand in opening the Wound. Respected, note, not obeyed. Demons do not obey. They are merely impressed. The lilitu Minagho kept Raliscrad, with a staff. Under them, the Templars of the Ivory Labyrinth , Baphomet's secret society, whose cells hide inside respectable families and who have infiltrated crusader orders before, and the cults of Deskari , Lord of the Locust Host, whose green-and-red locust wings are the sign of the enemy, and whose entire theology is that everything should be eaten. He has been at this since 4606. He is not in a hurry. Nobody who is winning is. Humans in the Wound. More than you would think, and fewer than there were. Cultists and mercenaries, who will tell you their employers keep their word; chattel kept for labour, food or sacrifice, in that order of preference; the willing workers of Raliscrad; the free folk of Gundrun; the enslaved miners of Iz, who broke every production record under the whip; and the thousands who lived in occupied Drezen. A character born inside the Wound is possible. Ask the DM. Ask nicely. What you meet on the road. Babau, brimoraks, vrocks, nabasu, shadow demons; derakni in the sky; vescavors, colony-swarms that eat metal and skin and are not particular about the order; plague steeds; urannags; the fungus called Shax's beard, which grows where it likes; ghouls in the chapels; the winter wraiths of the Crown of the World, raised from Drezen's own dead and still, in some sense, on the garrison rolls. And seventy-five years of names on the charts, every one of them marking a place someone was sure was safe. --- 7. Drezen: the city you are standing in What it was. A citadel-city raised in the hubris of the First Crusade, on the eastern arm of the Wounded Lands, a monument to the proposition that the war was as good as won. In 4638 it was betrayed from within by Staunton Vhane , a dwarf paladin turned, and taken by Aponavisius ; a wave of raw chaos turned most of its people into beast-things that ate each other, and the corpses were carted to the ice cliffs of the Crown of the World, where they rose as winter wraiths. Drezen's dead were put to work before they were cold. Saint Argil , the Pauper Sorcerer, died on the walls covering the flight of those who got out, and was made a saint for it, which is the crusade's pension scheme. The shrines of Iomedae and Sarenrae were made into mockeries of themselves, by demons who had clearly studied the originals. For seventy-five years, three generations, it was a demon city of some seven thousand. How it was taken. By a hundred volunteers and the four champions, in one week of Rova: the courtyard on the 8th, the sally port and the Templar's Court on the 9th, three days on the first floor of the citadel, Staunton Vhane dead and buried on the 12th and his glaive unmade in the forge, and the dungeons on the 13th, where the banner was. The enemy's catapults were wrecked during the siege, which nobody had thought to do in seventy-five years. Kamilo Dann , who held a dozen wounded in a stone gaol at Vilareth Ford until the ford was retaken, is one of many small heroes of that fortnight, and yours can be another. Small is a description of the pay. Who runs it now. In the sense that anyone does. - Irabeth Tirabade , half-orc paladin of the Eagle Watch, Governor. Held Kenabres together with her bare hands and was released by the Queen to ride north. On being handed Drezen: \"I had a city. It fell on me. Give me another.\" - Aron Kir , former spy, now Warden; keeps the citadel's watch and its secrets, and has the look of a man who has read your file. He has read your file. - Horgus Gwerm , a Kenabres noble who counted his gold in the rubble and now counts the city's grain. His office door reads: NO. Come in and I will explain why. - Anevia Tirabade , the Governor's wife and the crusade's agent; her spies map the west. - Joran Vhane , Staunton's kinsman, a dwarven master smith, surrendered in the citadel and now a penitent who dreams of relighting the forge beneath Drezen for good ends. Asked whether that was not what the last Vhane said, he answered, \"He said it better. I'm going to do it.\" For new arrivals, the Warden's office issues a single sheet: ON ARRIVAL IN DREZEN Where do I sleep? Where you can. The barracks are full. The inns are full. The temple compound has an orphanage, a food bank and an infirmary; qualify for one. Where do I eat? Rabiah's compound, the kitchen. Do not comment on the angle. Where do I get paid? The citadel, on completion, on presentation of the work, on the Warden's word that the work was done. The Warden's word is not for sale. Do not ask what it is for. Is the South Bank safe? The South Bank is across the river. Is the river safe? The river is dry. Is the dry river safe? Use the bridges. Who do I report a demon to? Anyone. Loudly. Who do I report a crusader to? The Warden. Quietly. Where is the Queen's steward? Nerosyan. He was here for an afternoon. We have his chair. The lie of the city. The citadel on the high ground, the Preservers' compound north-east of it, the temple compound and Rabiah's crooked ring quarter on the eastern ridge, the Celeste Market packed between the citadel wall and the dry riverbed, and across the riverbed the South Bank , scrap yards and rope-walks that spent three generations under demon rule and have not been searched. Cinder Row runs through it. That is where the work is. The rest is on the Map tab, and the map is in this document's voice, so you have been warned. The roads. East and south, the road down to Kenabres : Keeper's Canyon , where two armies camped, one of which is still there in pieces; Vilareth Ford ; about a week's march for a column, less for a rider. West, the road to the Hidden Temple : two days on foot, with nothing on it. \"Nothing\" is a technical term. Beyond that, the Gray Road , the Weeping Hills , the lava country, which the company will not be asked to visit, until it is. North, the Lake Lost to the Sun , named for the light that does not reach it. A hex on the Marchlands chart is twelve miles, or a hard day's march. What Drezen feels like. A garrison learning to be a town, and the town learning what a garrison thinks a town is for. Acid-scarred stone. Two temples facing each other across a street, both newer than the season. More buildings than people, and a standing policy of not finding out what is in them. Every party that goes west leaves by the western gate and is counted home through the same arch. The ledger at the gate has three columns: Out , In , and Shrinkage . It is the most complete record Drezen keeps. --- 8. Building the bio You do not need a life story. You need enough that the DM can hurt you with it. Think about all five of these, then fold two or three into a bio of a few paragraphs and leave the rest for the table to find out. - Where are you from? A quarter, a village, a cave, a boat. Who is still there, and do they know where you are? - What are you good at, and why? Not the class. The thing the class grew out of, and who paid for the lesson. - What are you afraid of, and why? Everyone in Mendev shares the same three fears. The fourth is the useful one, and it can be small. Bells. Being counted. - Why are you in Drezen right now? Not why you came. Why you are still here, this week, when the road south is open and nobody checks papers. - Where do you see yourself in five levels? Nobody in Drezen asks, because the answer is the cemetery. Ask anyway. A stall in the market. A squad. A skull carried home to a river that runs again. If it helps: one thing you carry that is not on the equipment list, and the gesture you make when you pass the temples. Naming by people - Native Mendevian : Iobarian roots, sturdy and slightly Slavic-sounding, the names of people who expected to be here a while. Hulrun Shappok, Irabeth Tirabade, Anevia, Sosiel Vaenic, Nestrin Alodae, Kamilo Dann, Trudean Delashaw, Jeskar Hinton, Tilda Merrow. - Crusader stock : southern names from anywhere: Taldan ( Galfrey, Aron Kir, Aldrich Thorne, Brannic Vayne, Dyre, Marcovina ), Chelaxian, Andoren, Varisian. A family name that is a place (\"of Lauterbury\") is a knightly affectation, and usually a place that has never heard of them. - Sarkorian / Kellid : short and hard: Dron, Gurog, Kronug, Belka, Inkit, Yala ; the clan name over the given name; longer and stranger for the notable: Uloric Dziergas, Domora Hume, Khraigorr Half-Face, Tarnshiav, Widowknife, Farheaven. Nobody in Sarkoris was called something they could not shout across a valley. - Mongrelmen of Neathholm : single, plain, given underground by people who did not expect anyone above to ask: Lann, Chusul, Opoli, Chyrrik. - Drezen-born : whatever the demons let them be called. Fenna Tusk is the one example, and she seems to have chosen it herself. --- 9. Sources, and what was left out Paizo. The Inner Sea World Guide ; Pathfinder Campaign Setting: The Worldwound ; Lost Kingdoms and Lost Cities of Golarion ; Wrath of the Righteous , The Worldwound Incursion and Sword of Valor ; Siege of the Diamond City ; Inner Sea Gods ; Lost Omens Gods & Magic ; Dragons Revisited ; Blood of Fiends ; the Pathfinder Society material on Jormurdun and the Starrise Spire. All checked against PathfinderWiki, September 2026. The Fifth Crusade. Its dramatis personae, lore, calendar and maps, the Book I to III source, and the secrets Following the Flame , The Ballad of the Wardstone's Champions and The Riftwardens' Children . Invented here, and yours to strike. The quoted lines from named people, the Preservers' recruiting bill, the pilgrims' pamphlet, the recruiters' sheet, the Warden's arrival sheet, the three kitchen sayings, the chair for Aroden at the Argent Shield, the grandmother's inventory, and the gate ledger's three columns. None of them contradict the chronicle. None of them are in it either. They become this table's canon when a chapter uses them, and not before. Deliberately excluded , because it has not happened yet or is not yet known at the table: everything Paizo has published about the years after 4713, the deep history of Threshold and Areelu Vorlesh, and Wrath of the Righteous Books IV to VI. If a player has read ahead, the bio should not show it."}];
